EM-A11-3","A-11","STU-01FE24BCI001","Team Member"],["MEM-A11-4","A-11","STU-01FE24BCI007","Team Member"],["MEM-A12-1","A-12","STU-01FE24BCI088","Team Lead"],["MEM-A12-2","A-12","STU-01FE24BCI041","Team Member"],["MEM-A12-3","A-12","STU-01FE24BCI031","Team Member"],["MEM-A12-4","A-12","STU-01FE24BCI075","Team Member"],["MEM-A15-1","A-15","STU-01FE24BCI035","Team Lead"],["MEM-A15-2","A-15","STU-01FE24BCI080","Team Member"],["MEM-A15-3","A-15","STU-01FE24BCI056","Team Member"],["MEM-A15-4","A-15","STU-01FE23BCI112","Team Member"],["MEM-A16-1","A-16","STU-01FE24BCI060","Team Lead"],["MEM-A16-2","A-16","STU-01FE24BCI086","Team Member"],["MEM-A16-3","A-16","STU-01FE24BCI077","Team Member"],["MEM-A16-4","A-16","STU-01FE24BCI053","Team Member"],["MEM-A18-1","A-18","STU-01FE24BCI004","Team Lead"],["MEM-A18-2","A-18","STU-01FE24BCU079","Team Member"],["MEM-A18-3","A-18","STU-NONE","Team Member"],["MEM-A18-4","A-18","STU-NONE","Team Member"],["MEM-B02-1","B-02","STU-01FE24BCI034","Team Lead"],["MEM-B02-2","B-02","STU-01FE24BCI018","Team Member"],["MEM-B02-3","B-02","STU-01FE24BCI027","Team Member"],["MEM-B02-4","B-02","STU-01FE24BCI025","Team Member"],["MEM-B09-1","B-09","STU-01FE24BCI022","Team Lead"],["MEM-B09-2","B-09","STU-01FE24BCI017","Team Member"],["MEM-B09-3","B-09","STU-01FE24BCI102","Team Member"],["MEM-B09-4","B-09","STU-01FE24BCI114","Team Member"]],"Projects":[["PRJ-A01","TEAM","A-01","Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support","In real-world applications, databases handle data creation, queries, modifications and security, but internal processes such as query processing, optimization, concurrency control and crash recovery often remain a black box to students. The proposed Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support aims to demonstrate these processes by implementing a SQL-like query processor, semantic validation, indexing, query execution and transaction management with commit, rollback and simulated crash recovery.","To implement a lexer, parser and semantic checker that convert SQL text into a validated parse tree and reject invalid queries with clear, position-aware error messages.\nTo design a storage engine that supports DML commands on the stored tables.\nTo build a query-execution engine that chooses an choosing algorithm for each query and returns correct results.\nTo implement transaction management with BEGIN, COMMIT and ROLLBACK, backed by an undo log, so that a failed or interrupted transaction leaves zero partial changes.\nTo demonstrate, through a command-line interface, how a single SQL statement moves from parsing through optimization and execution to a transactionally safe result.","Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA): Time-complexity (Big-O) analysis, searching, hashing and indexing structures used to compare and choose a query's access path  Hash indexing, B-Tree range indexing, binary search, Big-O comparison of scan vs index-lookup cost; Operating System Principles: Process/thread synchronization, locking (mutex/semaphore) for concurrent sessions, deadlock avoidance, file I/O, buffering, and crash recovery; Principles of Compiler Design (POCD): Lexical analysis, grammar (CFG), recursive-descent parsing, syntax-tree (AST) construction, and semantic analysis of SQL statements; Database Management Systems: Relational model, tables/tuples/attributes, primary/foreign keys and constraints, ACID properties, catalog management and normalization","https://github.com/topics/mini-rdbms-engine","","COMPLETED","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B04","TEAM","B-04","Multi-Process Search Engine with Persistent Index","Figure: Conceptual Workflow and System Architecture — PaperLens Multi-Process Search Engine","In today's digital world, large volumes of information are generated and stored in documents, files, and databases. Retrieving relevant information efficiently from this growing data is a major challenge. Traditional search systems often perform indexing and searching sequentially within a single process. As the volume of data increases, this approach can lead to higher execution time, inefficient resource utilization, and slower query responses.\n\nA Multi-Process Search Engine with Persistent Indexing aims to overcome these limitations by using multiple independent processes to perform indexing and searching concurrently.\n\nOur project, PaperLens, applies this idea to computer-science research papers: parallel reader processes prepare papers and citations, a persistent index stays consistent even after a crash, and search combines keyword, semantic and citation signals.","1. Build a multi-process pipeline that collects paper records and citations and prepares them in parallel.\n2. Design a persistent on-disk index that survives crashes and restarts without being left half-written.\n3. Use process synchronization and logging so several reader processes and one writer update the index safely.\n4. Create a search component that combines keyword, semantic and citation signals to return top-k results.\n5. Set up a process manager that recovers worker crashes automatically, and evaluate search quality.","Operating Systems: Multi-process design, inter-process communication (shared memory, queues), synchronization, process supervision and restart, file durability (fsync); Database Management Systems: Write-ahead logging, atomic commit, crash recovery, on-disk index organisation, consistent reads during writes, compaction; Data Structures and Algorithms: Inverted index, tokenization, BM25 ranking, heaps for top-k retrieval, hashing (Bloom filter, MinHash), graph ranking (PageRank); Computer Networks: HTTP communication with arXiv and OpenAlex, request rate limiting and retries, politeness towards public services; Discrete Mathematics: Graph theory (citation graph), set similarity (near-duplicate detection), probability behind hashing and ranking; Software Engineering: Modular multi-engine design, sprint-based delivery, requirement traceability, integration and crash testing","https://github.com/topics/multi-process-search-engine","","STAGE 1 REVISION","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B05","TEAM","B-05","Persistent Key-Value Storage Engine","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Persistent Key-Value Storage Engine","The proposed project is a Persistent Key-Value Storage System for storing and retrieving data using key-value pairs. Unlike temporary storage, the system aims to preserve stored data even after the application is closed or restarted. It provides a practical understanding of data storage, retrieval, and persistence.","To design and implement suitable data structures for organizing and efficiently accessing key-value pairs.\nTo develop a command interface with parsing and validation for handling basic key-value operations such as insertion, retrieval, updating, and deletion.\nTo develop a persistent storage mechanism that preserves key-value data across application restarts.\nTo implement mechanisms for maintaining data consistency and recovering from basic storage or application failures.\nTo integrate the developed components into a functional key-value storage engine and validate its correctness and performance.","Operating Systems: File Management, Storage Management, Process Management; Data Structures: Data Organization, Searching, Efficient Access; Design and Analysis of Algorithms: Searching, Updating, Operation Efficiency, Complexity Analysis; Database Management System: CRUD Operations","https://github.com/topics/persistent-key-value-engine","","STAGE 1 SUBMITTED","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A17","TEAM","A-17","Distributed File Metadata & Access Management Engine","Theme 1: Data Storage and Retrieval Systems — Distributed File Metadata & Access Management Engine","","","Theme 1: Data Storage and Retrieval Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A09","TEAM","A-09","Multi-Source Data Integration & Entity Resolution Engine","Theme 2: Data Processing and Analytics Systems — Multi-Source Data Integration & Entity Resolution Engine","","","Theme 2: Data Processing and Analytics Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B10","TEAM","B-10","Large-Scale Log Processing & Analytics Engine","Fig 1. Single-Machine Log Processing Pipeline Conceptual Workflow","Modern applications and servers generate log files that grow to many gigabytes. Each line is unstructured text, and the files often contain malformed lines and repeated entries. Because of this, the logs cannot be searched or analysed directly, and loading such a file fully into memory is not practical on an ordinary machine.\n\nThis project builds a single-machine pipeline that reads one massive log file efficiently, converts raw lines into structured records, removes bad and duplicate data, stores the clean records in a database and presents analytics on a web dashboard.","Read a very large single log file in newline-aligned chunks using memory mapping, without loading the whole file into RAM.\nParse each raw line into structured fields (timestamp, level, IP address, error code, message) and validate them, sending malformed lines to a quarantine store with a reason.\nRemove duplicate records with a bounded sliding-window fingerprint set so that memory use stays flat.\nStore clean records in PostgreSQL through in-memory micro-batches and bulk inserts, with retry on failed writes.\nProvide aggregate analytics (counts by level, error code and time) through a REST API with time-range and log-level filters, shown on a live web dashboard.","Operating Systems: Memory-mapped files (mmap), virtual memory and page cache, file descriptors, system calls, chunked I/O; Data Structures & Algorithms: Regular-expression parsing, hashing and fingerprints, hash sets, queues, sliding-window eviction, complexity analysis; Database Management Systems: Relational schema design, bulk insert, transactions, indexing, aggregate SQL, query tuning; Web Technologies: REST API design, HTTP and JSON, client-side charts, filtering through query parameters","https://github.com/topics/log-analytics-engine","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A08","TEAM","A-08","Large-Scale Dataset Profiling & Data Quality Engine","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Large-Scale Dataset Profiling & Data Quality Engine","Structured datasets can contain missing values, duplicate records, inconsistent types, invalid values, and statistically unusual observations. The proposed project brings dataset profiling and data-quality assessment into a single workflow so users can inspect a dataset, identify quality issues, select supported corrections, and revalidate the results. The presentations describe an upload-to-report workflow and emphasize preserving the original dataset while applying corrections to a separate copy.","To accept supported structured/tabular datasets and generate a structural profile.\nTo identify missing values, duplicate rows, data-type inconsistencies, and rule violations.\nTo compute descriptive statistics and flag potentially unusual observations.\nTo allow users to review detected issues and apply selected supported corrections without changing the original dataset.\nTo revalidate corrected data and present data-quality results and reports.","Database Management Systems: Dataset structure, schema, data types, consistency, storage and retrieval; Data Structures & Algorithm Analysis: Scanning, comparison/hashing for duplicate detection, efficient data processing; Probability & Statistics: Descriptive statistics, distributions, missing-value rates, IQR-based outlier screening; Machine Learning: Anomaly-analysis concepts and interpretation of unusual observations; Operating Systems: Files, memory, processing and resource limits; Web Technologies: Dataset upload, dashboard, user interactions and reporting","https://github.com/topics/data-quality-engine","","STAGE 1 REVISION","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A13","TEAM","A-13","Distributed Event Processing & Notification Engine","Theme 2: Data Processing and Analytics Systems — Distributed Event Processing & Notification Engine","","","Theme 2: Data Processing and Analytics Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A10","TEAM","A-10","Dependency-Aware Parallel Build System","Theme 3: System Software and Development Tools — Dependency-Aware Parallel Build System","","","Theme 3: System Software and Development Tools","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A14","TEAM","A-14","Mini-kernel with process, memory and file management","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini-kernel with process, memory and file management","Our project focuses on understanding how a computer can start and run its own basic operating-system kernel. A kernel is the core part of an operating system that manages access to hardware resources and provides essential services to programs. Developing even a small kernel involves several connected responsibilities, such as starting execution after boot, managing memory, handling processes, and organizing data on storage.\n\nOur proposed mini-kernel is a small x86-64 operating-system kernel developed from the ground up for learning and demonstration. It will be loaded by a bootloader and executed in an emulated computer environment during development. The project aims to implement selected kernel-level facilities and demonstrate how they work together. The work will use xv6 and other educational resources only for understanding operating-system concepts; the xv6 codebase will not be used as the project foundation.\n\nWe consider this a suitable Mini Project because it gives us an opportunity to study operating-system fundamentals through implementation rather than only theoretical discussion. A controlled scope will allow us to demonstrate the basic working of a kernel without attempting to build a complete commercial operating system.","To understand the role of a kernel in an operating system and study the basic stages involved in starting kernel execution.\nTo design and develop a small x86-64 kernel from scratch, using an existing bootloader as the initial loading mechanism.\nTo explore and implement selected fundamental kernel responsibilities related to process, memory and file management.\nTo demonstrate the kernel in an emulated hardware environment and observe its behaviour during execution.\nTo apply foundational Computer Science and system-software concepts through a practical, demonstrable operating-system project.","Operating System Principles: Kernel responsibilities, process management, memory management, file systems, interrupts and resource management; Computer Organization and Architecture: Processor basics, boot process, CPU operation, memory hierarchy and hardware interaction; Data Structures & Algorithms: Queues, lists, trees or other suitable structures, searching, scheduling concepts and efficient management of kernel data","https://github.com/topics/mini-kernel-os","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B07","TEAM","B-07","Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine","Java source programs pass through several compilation stages before they can be executed by the Java Virtual Machine. These stages include lexical analysis, syntax analysis, semantic checking, intermediate representation generation, code optimization and bytecode generation. Studying these stages separately can make it difficult to understand how they work together in a complete compiler.\n\nThe implemented project is a Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine for a defined Java subset. It uses a Flex-based lexer and a Bison parser based on a 53-rule grammar to process source programs and construct an Abstract Syntax Tree. The compiler performs semantic analysis using scoped symbol information, generates Three-Address Code (TAC), applies intermediate-code optimization passes, and uses a Java ASM backend to generate JVM class files. The C++ front end exports the optimized, type-annotated program as a typed intermediate representation (JSON), which the Java ASM backend reads to produce the class file. The generated class is then executed on the JVM, so the complete flow is organised as seven phases: scanner, parser, semantic analysis, TAC generation, optimization, bytecode generation and execution.\n\nThe project also provides a web-based interface for demonstrating the compilation phases and viewing their intermediate results, optimization information, generated bytecode and execution output. The implementation is limited to the language constructs supported by its grammar and backend; it is not a complete Java compiler.","To develop an integrated compiler pipeline that processes programs written using the supported Java language subset.\nTo implement lexical analysis, grammar-based syntax analysis, AST construction and semantic checking.\nTo generate Three-Address Code and apply intermediate-representation optimization techniques.\nTo generate JVM bytecode using the Java ASM backend, execute the generated class using the existing JVM and inspect the program output.\nTo provide a web-based demonstration interface through which users can inspect the major compilation phases and their results.","Principles of Compiler Design: Regular expressions and lexical patterns; tokens; Context-Free Grammar (CFG); BNF productions; Flex-based lexical analysis; Bison LALR(1) parsing; syntax-directed AST construction; semantic analysis; intermediate-code and bytecode generation.; Data Structures and Algorithms: Abstract Syntax Trees and tree traversal; scoped symbol tables and hash-based lookup; stacks for parser/scope processing; vectors and maps for tokens and IR; Three-Address Code; control-flow labels; optimization passes including constant folding, constant propagation, copy propagation, common subexpression elimination and dead-code/branch elimination.; Object-Oriented Programming: C++ classes and objects; structures and enumerations; inheritance and polymorphism where used in AST/compiler components; modular design and encapsulation.; Java, JVM and Web Technology: Java-based ASM backend; JVM class files, bytecode, operand stack and local variables; HTML, CSS and JavaScript interface; Python standard-library web server for displaying compilation phases and results.","https://github.com/topics/java-compiler-bytecode","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A02","TEAM","A-02","C Language Compiler & Code Generation Engine","Theme 3: System Software and Development Tools — C Language Compiler & Code Generation Engine","","","Theme 3: System Software and Development Tools","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B01","TEAM","B-01","Incremental Build & Dependency Resolution Tool","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Incremental Build and Dependency Resolution Tool","Software projects often contain multiple build actions that depend on one another. When a source file changes, running every build action again can repeat work that is not required. Developers need a reliable way to identify the affected actions, respect dependency order and reuse previously generated outputs only when those outputs are still valid.\n\nThe proposed Incremental Build and Dependency Resolution Tool addresses this problem through file fingerprinting, dependency-graph analysis, build planning, task execution and artifact caching. The extended prototype also explores controlled execution of suitable independent actions on trusted computers connected through a local-area network (LAN).","To create a project manifest describing build actions, commands, inputs, outputs and dependencies.\nTo detect added, modified, deleted and unchanged files using SHA-256 fingerprints, and identify the build actions affected by those changes.\nTo represent build actions as a directed dependency graph, detect dependency cycles and determine a valid execution order.\nTo schedule and execute eligible actions as operating-system processes with bounded local parallelism, and investigate controlled TCP-based execution on trusted LAN workers.\nTo reuse cached artifacts only when the complete declared action identity and expected outputs are valid, while persistently maintaining file state, artifact metadata and build history.\nTo evaluate correctness and performance by comparing clean builds, incremental builds, cache reuse, and local versus networked execution.","Data Structures and Algorithms / Design and Analysis of Algorithms: Directed graphs, adjacency lists, hash maps/sets, BFS/DFS, topological sorting and cycle detection; Operating Systems: File and process operations, process exit codes, worker pools, synchronization, concurrency limits and task-state management; Database Management Systems: SQLite tables, keys, CRUD operations, transactions, build history and persistent metadata; Computer Networks: TCP client-server communication, message framing, worker registration, heartbeats, timeouts, task dispatch and artifact transfer; Object-Oriented Programming: Interfaces and classes for adapters, actions, scheduler, cache, state repository, coordinator and workers","https://github.com/topics/incremental-build-system","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B13","TEAM","B-13","Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine","In a multi-peer file distribution environment, a file may be divided into smaller portions and distributed among multiple participating peers for storage and transfer. Successful retrieval of the complete file therefore depends on the availability and accessibility of the required portions across the participating peers.\n\nIn practical distributed environments, peers may become temporarily unavailable due to process termination, network interruption or other failures. Such unavailability can make required file portions inaccessible and may interrupt file retrieval or prevent successful reconstruction. Repeatedly depending on the same unavailable peer can further increase recovery time and communication overhead.\n\nThe proposed Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine aims to study and develop a system that can continue file distribution and retrieval despite defined peer failures. The project focuses on identifying unavailable data, obtaining the required information from available participating peers, verifying recovered data and reconstructing the original file.","To develop a multi-peer file distribution system capable of dividing and distributing file data among participating peers.\nTo enable the system to identify unavailable or failed peers and determine the impact on ongoing file retrieval.\nTo provide a recovery mechanism that can retrieve missing file data from available surviving replicas on participating peers and reconstruct the original file.\nTo verify recovered data and successfully reconstruct the requested file after supported peer failures.\nTo study the behaviour of the system under different controlled peer availability and failure conditions.","Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA): Chunking, distribution strategy, replica/source selection, searching, ordering and reconstruction.; Computer Networks: Peer communication, data transfer, addressing, connection management and network failures.; Operating Systems: Processes, process lifecycle, file handling, resource management and process failure.; Distributed Systems: Peer-based resource sharing, replication, fault tolerance, availability and failure handling.; Database Management Systems (DBMS): Metadata storage, chunk-to-peer mapping, peer status and efficient metadata lookup.; Object-Oriented Programming (OOP): Modular system design using components such as Peer, Chunk, Transfer and Recovery.","https://github.com/topics/p2p-file-distribution-engine","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B14","TEAM","B-14","Adaptive Video Streaming & Buffer Management Engine","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools — Adaptive Video Streaming & Buffer Management Engine","","","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A07","TEAM","A-07","Network Traffic Monitoring & Protocol Analysis Tool","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini-Shark Network Traffic Monitoring & Protocol Analysis Tool","Network administrators use packet-capture utilities, connectivity tests and interface statistics to understand network behaviour. However, a packet capture taken at only one location gives limited visibility into what happens along the complete network path. An observation at the sending point cannot by itself show whether the same traffic was delayed, changed or failed to appear at a later observation point. Synthetic tests such as ICMP ping may also not represent the behaviour of real application traffic. In addition, heavy traffic can make monitoring itself more difficult because capture and processing may not keep up with incoming packets.\n\nMini-Shark is proposed as a network traffic monitoring and protocol analysis tool that observes live traffic at more than one point in a controlled network, compares the observations and presents useful diagnostic information. It aims to help an operator investigate transit delay and unmatched observations while making clear that a missing observation may also result from capture or telemetry limitations, rather than automatically proving network-path loss. The proposed work will be demonstrated in a controlled virtual laboratory.","To study how network traffic can be observed at multiple points along a communication path.\nTo develop a tool that captures and analyses selected live network traffic for monitoring purposes.\nTo compare packet observations from different points and estimate transit delay where timestamps are comparable.\nTo identify unmatched observations for further investigation without treating them automatically as confirmed network loss.\nTo evaluate the monitoring tool under normal, concurrent, delayed and high-traffic test conditions.","Computer Networks: Network communication, Ethernet and IP addressing, TCP/UDP, routing, packet behaviour and network performance.; Operating System Principles: Network interfaces, packet capture, processes and threads, producer-consumer concepts, buffering and resource management.; Data Structures & Algorithms: Efficient organization and comparison of packet observations, searching, matching and time-based processing concepts.; Database Management Systems: Organizing, storing and retrieving probe observations, monitoring events and diagnostic summaries.","https://github.com/topics/network-traffic-monitoring","","STAGE 1 SUBMITTED","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A05","TEAM","A-05","Distributed Peer Search & File Sharing Engine","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools — Distributed Peer Search & File Sharing Engine","","","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B11","TEAM","B-11","DNS Resolver & Caching Server","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools — DNS Resolver & Caching Server","","","Theme 4: Networked Systems and Tools","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B12","TEAM","B-12","HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching","When several users on the same network access the same web resource, each request may travel to the origin server even when the resource has not changed. This repeated communication increases response time and uses network bandwidth unnecessarily. In places such as college labs, hostels and shared networks, many users may request the same pages or static resources.\n\nOur proposed HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching is intended to reduce this repetition by placing a proxy between the client and the origin server. The proxy receives a request and checks whether a suitable, fresh response is already available in its memory. If it is available, the response can be returned to the client without contacting the origin server again. If it is not available or is no longer fresh, the proxy contacts the origin server and forwards the response to the client.","To study how repeated requests for unchanged web resources affect response time and network bandwidth.\nTo develop an HTTP proxy that accepts client requests and communicates with the required origin server.\nTo reuse suitable cached responses for repeated requests while ensuring that only fresh responses are served.\nTo reduce unnecessary communication with origin servers and study the effect of caching on response time and bandwidth usage.\nTo apply Computer Networks, algorithm design and Operating Systems concepts in a practical networking system and evaluate its behaviour under multiple client requests.","Computer Networks: Client-server communication, TCP sockets, ports, HTTP request-response flow and proxy communication.; Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA): Efficient searching, average-case lookup, cache replacement decisions and time/space complexity.; Operating System Principles: Processes and threads, concurrent request handling, synchronization, shared resources and resource management.; Software Engineering: Requirement analysis, modular development, testing, benchmarking, documentation and version control.","https://github.com/topics/http-proxy-cache","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A06","TEAM","A-06","Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling","Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling","Preparing an academic timetable involves coordinating courses, sections, faculty members, classrooms, laboratories and time slots while satisfying multiple interdependent constraints. Factors such as faculty and room availability, prescribed weekly teaching hours, fixed breaks and consecutive laboratory sessions make the scheduling process particularly complex.\n\nManual or iterative scheduling often requires repeated conflict checks, as resolving one conflict may introduce another. A faculty member may be assigned to overlapping classes, a section may receive two courses in the same time slot, or a room may be allocated to more than one class simultaneously. These conflicts can require repeated timetable revisions and make it difficult to maintain consistent schedules for all stakeholders.\n\nThe proposed Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling Engine aims to develop a structured system that models academic entities and scheduling rules, generates timetables that satisfy mandatory constraints, considers preferential requirements where feasible, and reports diagnostic information when no feasible schedule can be produced. The primary stakeholders are the Timetable Coordinator, Department Head (HoD), faculty members, and students/sections. Secondary stakeholders include room and laboratory coordinators and academic administration.","To develop a structured data model for courses, sections, faculty members, classrooms, laboratories and scheduling time slots.\nTo identify, classify and represent mandatory (hard) and preferential (soft) scheduling constraints.\nTo generate academic timetables that satisfy all mandatory constraints and eliminate resource-allocation conflicts.\nTo incorporate faculty availability and workload considerations into the scheduling process.\nTo provide consistent and synchronized timetable views for classes, faculty members and rooms.\nTo generate meaningful conflict information and diagnostic feedback when no feasible timetable can be produced.","Data Structures and Algorithms: Backtracking, search strategies, conflict detection and optimization techniques.; Database Management Systems (DBMS): Relational data modelling, SQL, CRUD operations, transaction management and scheduling records.; Discrete Mathematics: Sets, relations, propositional logic, combinatorial modelling and constraint satisfaction problems (CSPs).; Object-Oriented Programming (OOP): Classes, objects, encapsulation, abstraction and modular software design.; Operating Systems: Scheduling principles, resource allocation and resource-conflict management.; Software Engineering: Requirements analysis, UML modelling, feasibility assessment and software testing.","https://github.com/topics/academic-timetable-scheduler","","STAGE 2 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A04","TEAM","A-04","Resource Packing & Allocation Optimization Engine","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems — Resource Packing & Allocation Optimization Engine","","","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B03","TEAM","B-03","Route Planning & Network Optimization Engine","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems — Route Planning & Network Optimization Engine","","","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B06","TEAM","B-06","Dynamic Task Assignment & Scheduling Engine","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems — Dynamic Task Assignment & Scheduling Engine","","","Theme 5: Algorithmic and Optimization Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A03","TEAM","A-03","Smart Campus Lab and Resource Optimizer","Conceptual Flow - Smart Campus Lab & Resource Optimizer","In many colleges, academic timetables and resource allocation are still managed manually, making it difficult to handle sudden changes, additional classes, and faculty unavailability. These challenges often result in scheduling conflicts, underutilized classrooms and laboratories, and uneven faculty workloads. Managing multiple scheduling requests is also complicated due to the lack of a systematic approach to prioritizing requests, ensuring fairness, and considering resource availability. Therefore, an efficient system is needed to simplify timetable management, optimize the use of existing resources, and ensure smooth academic activities with minimal disruption.","To detect overlapping booking requests and resource conflicts accurately.\nTo allocate resources while following capacity, time-slot and eligibility rules.\nTo improve resource utilization and fairness compared with first-come-first-served allocation.\nTo identify and report requests that cannot be fulfilled when resources are limited.\nTo generate reports on resource utilization, fairness and conflicts.\nTo test the system with increasing numbers of booking requests.","Design & Analysis of Algorithms: Greedy algorithms, Dynamic Programming and Backtracking; Data Structures & Algorithms: Trees, heaps, graphs and interval-based conflict detection; Operating Systems: Concurrency, process scheduling, locks and resource coordination; Discrete Mathematical Structures: Sets, relations, graphs and constraint modelling; Software Engineering: Modular design, requirements and system testing; Database Management Systems: Booking records, data storage, transactions and data consistency","https://github.com/topics/campus-resource-optimizer","","COMPLETED","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A11","TEAM","A-11","Distributed Job Queue & Task Processing Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Distributed Job Queue & Task Processing Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A12","TEAM","A-12","Concurrent Booking & Resource Allocation Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Concurrent Booking & Resource Allocation Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A15","TEAM","A-15","Concurrent Service Request & Dispatch Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Concurrent Service Request & Dispatch Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A16","TEAM","A-16","Multi-User Resource Reservation & Scheduling Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Multi-User Resource Reservation & Scheduling Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-A18","TEAM","A-18","Concurrent Helpdesk Ticketing & Service Dispatch System","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Concurrent Helpdesk Ticketing & Service Dispatch System","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B02","TEAM","B-02","Transaction-Safe Multi-User Booking & Waitlist Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Transaction-Safe Multi-User Booking & Waitlist Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["PRJ-B09","TEAM","B-09","Multi-User Resource Reservation & Scheduling Engine","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems — Multi-User Resource Reservation & Scheduling Engine","","","Theme 6: Concurrent and Transactional Systems","","","STAGE 0 DRAFT","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"]],"StageSubmissions":[["SUB-A01-0","PRJ-A01",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support\",\"problem\":\"In real-world applications, databases handle data creation, queries, modifications and security, but internal processes such as query processing, optimization, concurrency control and crash recovery often remain a black box to students. The proposed Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support aims to demonstrate these processes by implementing a SQL-like query processor, semantic validation, indexing, query execution and transaction management with commit, rollback and simulated crash recovery.\",\"objectives\":[\"To implement a lexer, parser and semantic checker that convert SQL text into a validated parse tree and reject invalid queries with clear, position-aware error messages.\",\"To design a storage engine that supports DML commands on the stored tables.\",\"To build a query-execution engine that chooses an choosing algorithm for each query and returns correct results.\",\"To implement transaction management with BEGIN, COMMIT and ROLLBACK, backed by an undo log, so that a failed or interrupted transaction leaves zero partial changes.\",\"To demonstrate, through a command-line interface, how a single SQL statement moves from parsing through optimization and execution to a transactionally safe result.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA)\",\"concepts\":\"Time-complexity (Big-O) analysis, searching, hashing and indexing structures used to compare and choose a query's access path  Hash indexing, B-Tree range indexing, binary search, Big-O comparison of scan vs index-lookup cost\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Operating System Principles\",\"concepts\":\"Process/thread synchronization, locking (mutex/semaphore) for concurrent sessions, deadlock avoidance, file I/O, buffering, and crash recovery\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Principles of Compiler Design (POCD)\",\"concepts\":\"Lexical analysis, grammar (CFG), recursive-descent parsing, syntax-tree (AST) construction, and semantic analysis of SQL statements\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Relational model, tables/tuples/attributes, primary/foreign keys and constraints, ACID properties, catalog management and normalization\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Build a lexer, parser and semantic checker that reject invalid queries with clear, beginner-friendly error messages (what is wrong, where, and a suggested fix).\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Parse and execute a bounded SQL-like subset — CREATE TABLE (typed columns, PRIMARY KEY, NOT NULL), INSERT, SELECT with WHERE, UPDATE and DELETE — on tables stored by the engine.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Create and use a hash or range index on a column, and choose between index lookup, index range scan and full table scan for each query.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Support BEGIN / COMMIT / ROLLBACK transactions with undo logging, safe-preview confirmation for UPDATE/DELETE, and recovery after a simulated crash.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Full ANSI SQL standard support (multi-table joins, subqueries, stored procedures, triggers)\",\"reason\":\"A complete SQL standard is too large for a Mini Project, a bounded, documented grammar keeps parsing and semantic analysis achievable.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Distributed database execution, replication and cloud deployment\",\"reason\":\"This would introduce multi-node networking and distributed-consistency complexity beyond the project's single-node scope.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Advanced concurrency (MVCC, multiple isolation levels) and a cost-based query optimizer\",\"reason\":\"The project uses a simpler locking and undo-log model, a full cost-based optimizer and multi-version concurrency need sophistication beyond this timeline.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"The project is achievable as a 3-credit Mini Project if the SQL grammar is bounded, execution stays single-node, and the transaction model uses simple locking with undo-log based recovery rather than a full production RDBMS.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"Standard laptops, a general-purpose programming language with file-handling support, and local file storage for tables, the catalog and the transaction log. No special hardware or paid database services are required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic programming, Database Management Systems, Design & Analysis of Algorithms (indexing/searching), Operating System Principles (concurrency/file I/O), and Principles of Compiler Design (lexing/parsing).\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Correctness of a hand-built parser and recovery mechanism, and the performance limits of a single-node, in-process engine.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Standard language and file-I/O libraries may be used for peripheral tasks such as the command-line interface, but the core lexer, parser, query executor, indexing and transaction/recovery logic will be designed and implemented by the team rather than using an existing database engine.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with scope modification\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini RDBMS Engine with Query Processing & Transaction Support\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team01_conceptual_workflow.jpg\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Jyothi Patil","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A01-1","PRJ-A01",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Tokenize & parsing: The system shall split each query into tokens and build a parse tree (AST) using a fixed grammar, and reject any query that breaks the grammar.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-1, SN-3\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Semantic checking: The system shall check that every table and column exists and every value matches its column type before any data is touched.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-4\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Query error reporting: For every rejected query, the system shall show what went wrong, where (token / position) and a hint to fix it.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-4\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Table Management, Data Manipulation and Indexing: The system shall support table creation, validated record insertion, retrieval using SELECT and WHERE conditions, updating and deleting records, and creating and maintaining hash and sorted indexes consistently across INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and ROLLBACK operations.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-2, SN-3, SN-7\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Choose access path: For each SELECT / UPDATE / DELETE, the system shall choose index lookup, index range or full scan, picking the option that reads the fewest rows.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-7\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"EXPLAIN (learning mode): When the user types EXPLAIN <query>, the system shall show the tokens, parse tree, chosen plan and the reason for the choice, without changing any data.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-1\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"Safe UPDATE / DELETE preview: Before an UPDATE or DELETE runs, the system shall show how many rows will be affected and ask for Y/N confirmation, with an extra warning when there is no WHERE clause.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-5\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"BEGIN / COMMIT / ROLLBACK: The system shall support BEGIN, COMMIT and ROLLBACK, a single statement outside BEGIN shall run as its own transaction (auto-commit).\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-6\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"Undo log & rollback: The system shall record the old value of every change before applying it, and on ROLLBACK or any error shall restore all of them so no partial change remains.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-6\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"Saving & reloading data: The system shall save committed tables to files on disk and load them again when it starts.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-8, SN-9\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-11\",\"desc\":\"Crash recovery: If the program stops in the middle of a transaction, on restart the system shall keep only committed data and discard unfinished changes.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-8\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-12\",\"desc\":\"Concurrency control: The system shall use shared / exclusive table locks so two sessions cannot corrupt the same table, and shall detect a deadlock (or 2 s timeout) and abort one transaction.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-8\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-13\",\"desc\":\"Command-line interface: The system shall accept queries ending with ';' (also multi-line), show results as a table with row count and time taken, and show errors without crashing.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"SN-1, SN-9\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Efficient query processing\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Indexed equality lookup on 10,000 rows ≤ 1 ms; full scan ≤ 50 ms; parse and plan ≤ 10 ms.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Efficient handling of larger datasets\",\"cat\":\"Scalability\",\"target\":\"Support up to 100,000 rows per table; peak memory ≤ 3× raw data size.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Atomic and reliable transactions\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"Zero partial changes in 50/50 rollback and failure-injection tests.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Consistent and accurate data handling\",\"cat\":\"Correctness\",\"target\":\"Index and full-scan results identical in 100% of test queries; 100% of rule violations rejected.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Secure input handling\",\"cat\":\"Security\",\"target\":\"Queries exceeding 1,000 characters rejected; identifiers validated; no eval/exec of user input; 20/20 negative tests pass.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Safe concurrent access by multiple sessions\",\"cat\":\"Concurrency\",\"target\":\"0 lost updates with 4 sessions × 100 updates; deadlocks resolved within 2 seconds.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Clear and beginner-friendly error messages\",\"cat\":\"Usability\",\"target\":\"100% of error messages indicate what went wrong, where and provide a hint; at least 8/10 student testers resolve errors using only the message.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"Portable and lightweight execution\",\"cat\":\"Portability\",\"target\":\"Python 3.10+ standard library only; runs on Windows and Linux; setup ≤ 5 minutes.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants to create a table with typed columns and rules so the data has structure.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types CREATE TABLE students(id INT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, age INT) → query parsed and checked → table saved in catalog → 'Table students created'.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Table already exists or unknown type → error with hint, nothing created.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants to add a record and be sure it follows the table rules.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types INSERT INTO students VALUES (1,'Asha',19) → types and rules checked → row stored, index updated → '1 row inserted'.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Duplicate id / NULL name / text in INT column → row rejected, nothing stored, clear message.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants to get only the rows needed, using WHERE conditions.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types SELECT name FROM students WHERE age >= 18 → planner picks index or scan → matching rows shown with count and time.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Empty table or no match → '0 rows', unknown column → error 'Did you mean age?'.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants to change data but see what will change first.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types UPDATE students SET age=20 WHERE id=5 → preview '1 row will change. Continue? (Y/N)' → Y → row changed, '1 row updated'.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"N → nothing changes, no WHERE → extra warning 'EVERY row will change'.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants to delete only what is meant to be deleted.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types DELETE FROM students WHERE age < 18 → preview count and confirm → rows removed, count shown.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"N → nothing deleted, no WHERE → extra warning, 0 matches → '0 rows deleted'.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"User wants several changes saved together, or undone together if something goes wrong.\",\"mainFlow\":\"BEGIN → runs 2-3 INSERT / UPDATE statements → COMMIT → all changes saved to disk together, or ROLLBACK → table exactly as before BEGIN.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"COMMIT / ROLLBACK without BEGIN → 'No active transaction', a statement fails inside the transaction → whole transaction rolled back automatically.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Developer wants to see how a query runs and to speed up frequent searches with an index.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM students WHERE id = 7 → sees tokens, parse tree, chosen plan and reason, no data changed, or types CREATE INDEX ON students(age) USING SORTED → index built from existing rows.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid query → shows the exact step (lexer / parser / checker) where it failed; column does not exist → error, nothing built.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Developer wants to know exactly why a query was rejected.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Types SELEC * FROM students → sees 'Error at position 1: unknown keyword SELEC. Did you mean SELECT?' → fixes and re-runs.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Several mistakes in one query → first one reported with position, CLI keeps running (no crash).\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"System Admin\",\"desc\":\"Admin needs only committed data to remain after a crash, and two sessions on the same table must not corrupt data.\",\"mainFlow\":\"System restarts → finds unfinished transaction → discards it → loads last committed data → 'Recovered', Session A updates students (exclusive lock) → Session B waits → A commits, lock released → B continues.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Data file damaged → load last good copy, report affected table, deadlock (A waits for B, B waits for A) → detected, one transaction aborted and rolled back, message shown.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"CREATE TABLE students(id INT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, age INT) adds the table with 3 typed columns to the catalog, running it again gives a 'table exists' error.\",\"method\":\"Unit test + CLI demo\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"A valid INSERT increases row count by 1. Duplicate id, NULL name and 'abc' into INT are each rejected (3 / 3) and the row count does not change.\",\"method\":\"Unit test (pytest)\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"20 SELECT test queries return exactly the same rows as the expected answers (checked against SQLite as reference): 20 / 20.\",\"method\":\"Comparison test vs SQLite\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"40 valid queries produce a parse tree; 20 invalid queries are rejected: 60 / 60 correct.\",\"method\":\"Parser test suite\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"After 1,000 random INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE / ROLLBACK operations, index results = full-scan results for every key.\",\"method\":\"Randomised consistency test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"BEGIN; execute 3 INSERTs; COMMIT → all 3 rows are successfully committed. A single statement outside BEGIN runs as its own transaction\",\"method\":\"Integration test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-10\",\"criteria\":\"Committed tables are saved to disk and successfully reloaded after restart. A single statement outside BEGIN is also saved immediately.\",\"method\":\"Persistence and restart test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"BEGIN; INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE; ROLLBACK → table checksum equals the value before BEGIN in 50 / 50 runs.\",\"method\":\"Failure-injection test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Benchmark on 10,000 rows: indexed lookup ≤ 1 ms, full scan ≤ 50 ms.\",\"method\":\"benchmark.py\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team01_use_case_diagram.jpg?v=20261104\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Jyothi Patil","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A01-2","PRJ-A01",2,"Approved","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","Architecture, data design, and algorithmic modeling approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Jyothi Patil","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A01-3","PRJ-A01",3,"Approved","{\"implementation\":\"Modular engine pipeline implemented following System Design boundaries.\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/mini-rdbms-engine\",\"challenges\":\"Handled concurrency and edge-case validation across module boundaries.\"}","Core implementation modules verified.",9,"{}","Ms. Jyothi Patil","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A01-4","PRJ-A01",4,"Approved","{\"testing\":\"Acceptance test suite covering all FR and NFR targets.\",\"test_results\":\"All acceptance verification checks passed.\",\"metrics\":\"Zero hard-constraint conflicts; target response latency met.\",\"evaluation\":\"Ready for final viva examination.\"}","Final validation and acceptance evidence approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Jyothi Patil","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B04-0","PRJ-B04",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Multi-Process Search Engine with Persistent Index\",\"problem\":\"In today's digital world, large volumes of information are generated and stored in documents, files, and databases. Retrieving relevant information efficiently from this growing data is a major challenge. Traditional search systems often perform indexing and searching sequentially within a single process. As the volume of data increases, this approach can lead to higher execution time, inefficient resource utilization, and slower query responses.\\n\\nA Multi-Process Search Engine with Persistent Indexing aims to overcome these limitations by using multiple independent processes to perform indexing and searching concurrently.\\n\\nOur project, PaperLens, applies this idea to computer-science research papers: parallel reader processes prepare papers and citations, a persistent index stays consistent even after a crash, and search combines keyword, semantic and citation signals.\",\"objectives\":[\"1. Build a multi-process pipeline that collects paper records and citations and prepares them in parallel.\",\"2. Design a persistent on-disk index that survives crashes and restarts without being left half-written.\",\"3. Use process synchronization and logging so several reader processes and one writer update the index safely.\",\"4. Create a search component that combines keyword, semantic and citation signals to return top-k results.\",\"5. Set up a process manager that recovers worker crashes automatically, and evaluate search quality.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Multi-process design, inter-process communication (shared memory, queues), synchronization, process supervision and restart, file durability (fsync)\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Write-ahead logging, atomic commit, crash recovery, on-disk index organisation, consistent reads during writes, compaction\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Data Structures and Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Inverted index, tokenization, BM25 ranking, heaps for top-k retrieval, hashing (Bloom filter, MinHash), graph ranking (PageRank)\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Computer Networks\",\"concepts\":\"HTTP communication with arXiv and OpenAlex, request rate limiting and retries, politeness towards public services\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Discrete Mathematics\",\"concepts\":\"Graph theory (citation graph), set similarity (near-duplicate detection), probability behind hashing and ranking\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"6\",\"course\":\"Software Engineering\",\"concepts\":\"Modular multi-engine design, sprint-based delivery, requirement traceability, integration and crash testing\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Harvesting CS papers from arXiv and citations from OpenAlex for a bounded subject\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Persistent on-disk index with logging and crash recovery\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Parallel readers with synchronized single-writer updates\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Keyword, semantic and citation ranking with top-k query and evaluation\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Full text of millions of papers\",\"reason\":\"Too heavy for one semester; titles and abstracts are used.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Production-grade UI/UX\",\"reason\":\"The project focuses on core system design and algorithms, so a command-line interface is enough.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Distributed multi-machine processing\",\"reason\":\"The theme targets multi-process design on a single system rather than multi-machine networking.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with controlled scope: a bounded set of CS papers (one subject area and date range) and a few reader processes. Multi-process ingestion, crash-safe persistence, indexing and hybrid ranking can be demonstrated without large-scale deployment.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"Student laptop/PC with 8+ GB RAM, enough disk space and internet access; Python 3.10+, a free local embedding model, and open data from arXiv and OpenAlex.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic Operating Systems, DBMS, Data Structures and Algorithms, Computer Networks, Discrete Mathematics and Python; processes, IPC, logging and recovery, hashing, graphs and basic information retrieval.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Limited computing power and bandwidth cap the number of papers and processes; arXiv and OpenAlex rate limits apply; abstracts alone may limit ranking quality; crash behaviour needs deliberate fault-injection tests.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Libraries may be used for HTTP, parsing, embeddings and arrays. Multi-process coordination, logging and recovery, index storage, BM25, rank fusion and citation ranking will be implemented by the students.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Figure: Conceptual Workflow and System Architecture — PaperLens Multi-Process Search Engine\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team04_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Umadevi F M","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B04-1","PRJ-B04",1,"Revision requested","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR1\",\"desc\":\"The system shall collect paper records and citation links from arXiv and OpenAlex for chosen categories, within the sources' limits (at most one arXiv request per 3 seconds) and resumable after a stop. [E1]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 1 In scope (§5.1): 1 Constraints (§6): arXiv and OpenAlex rate limits; limited bandwidth caps the number of papers Libraries (§6): HTTP and parsing libraries allowed Out of scope (§5.2): 1 (titles and abstracts only)\"},{\"id\":\"FR2\",\"desc\":\"The system shall process papers in parallel reader processes and store them through a single writer; crashed or hung workers are restarted so that every paper is stored exactly once. [E1]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 1, 3, 5 In scope (§5.1): 1, 3 Constraints (§6): limited bandwidth and computing power cap papers and processes; crash behaviour needs fault-injection tests Libraries (§6): multi-process coordination written by the team Out of scope (§5.2): 3 (single machine only)\"},{\"id\":\"FR3\",\"desc\":\"The system shall store each acknowledged paper durably (diary, immutable segments, atomic MANIFEST), recover automatically after a crash without loss or duplicates, and merge small segments in the background without affecting queries. [E2]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 2, 3 In scope (§5.1): 2 Constraints (§6): crash behaviour needs fault-injection tests; limited computing power caps index size Libraries (§6): logging, recovery and index storage written by the team Out of scope (§5.2): 3 (single machine only)\"},{\"id\":\"FR4\",\"desc\":\"The system shall rank papers for a query by keyword search (BM25 over an inverted index) and by semantic search (compressed meaning vectors). [E3, vector storage by E2]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 4 In scope (§5.1): 4 Constraints (§6): limited computing power caps papers; abstracts alone may limit ranking quality Libraries (§6): embedding library allowed; BM25 and compressed vector search written by the team Out of scope (§5.2): 1 (titles and abstracts only)\"},{\"id\":\"FR5\",\"desc\":\"The system shall merge the keyword and semantic results with reciprocal rank fusion and rerank the top results with citation scores (PageRank), with optional category and year filters. [E3]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 4 In scope (§5.1): 4 Constraints (§6): OpenAlex rate limits; abstracts alone may limit ranking quality Libraries (§6): rank fusion and citation ranking written by the team\"},{\"id\":\"FR6\",\"desc\":\"The system shall show each result with title, authors, year, snippet and a plain-language reason, with near-duplicate papers removed (MinHash), through a command-line interface. [E3, all engines]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 4 In scope (§5.1): 4 Out of scope (§5.2): 2 (command line only, no production UI)\"},{\"id\":\"FR7\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide a one-command evaluation reporting nDCG@10, Recall@100, latency and index size for each search setup. [E3]\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 5 In scope (§5.1): 4 Constraints (§6): limited computing power caps the size of the evaluation\"},{\"id\":\"FR8\",\"desc\":\"The system may rerank the top ~100 candidates with word-level vectors and offer “Key papers” and “More like this” views. [E3]\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Objectives (§2): 4 In scope (§5.1): 4 Constraints (§6): limited computing power (only the top ~100 candidates are reranked) Libraries (§6): embedding library allowed\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR1\",\"desc\":\"Every acknowledged paper is preserved without loss, duplication, or partial storage, even if any process is terminated at any stage of execution.\",\"cat\":\"Durability and consistency — E2 DBMS (with E1)\",\"target\":\"0 lost and 0 duplicated papers after a kill at each of 6 write steps, 12 random SIGKILLs and a writer kill mid-ingest; fsck reports 0 errors\"},{\"id\":\"NFR2\",\"desc\":\"Ingestion finishes correctly while worker processes are being killed.\",\"cat\":\"Fault tolerance — E1 OS\",\"target\":\"With a reader killed every 0.25 s and the writer killed twice, every paper ends up stored once; a dead worker is replaced within 5 s\"},{\"id\":\"NFR3\",\"desc\":\"Ingestion throughput grows with the number of reader processes.\",\"cat\":\"Scalability — E1 OS\",\"target\":\"At least 70% parallel efficiency at 4 readers on a machine with 4 or more cores; speed-up curve for 1, 2, 4 and 8 readers reported\"},{\"id\":\"NFR4\",\"desc\":\"A hybrid query answers fast on a laptop.\",\"cat\":\"Performance — E3 Retrieval (storage by E2)\",\"target\":\"95th-percentile latency <= 500 ms at 50,000 papers, measured over 300 queries (without the optional word-level rerank)\"},{\"id\":\"NFR5\",\"desc\":\"Compressed vectors save memory without hurting search.\",\"cat\":\"Resource efficiency — E3 Retrieval (with E2)\",\"target\":\"Stored codes <= 1/8 of float32 size; recall@10 of compressed-plus-rescored search >= 95% of exact search\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC1\",\"actor\":\"Index Operator\",\"desc\":\"Operator harvests new papers for chosen arXiv categories.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Operator starts the harvester with categories and a start date -> system requests pages politely (3 s apart, one connection) -> records are saved to a file -> progress is saved so the run can resume\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Server asks to wait (503 + Retry-After) -> wait and retry; resumption token expires -> restart from newest saved datestamp\"},{\"id\":\"UC2\",\"actor\":\"Index Operator\",\"desc\":\"Operator ingests harvested papers into the index.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Operator starts ingest with N readers -> supervisor sends papers to readers -> readers analyse and build vectors -> the single writer logs, commits and acknowledges -> buffer flushed to a segment and MANIFEST updated\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Same paper arrives twice -> second copy is ignored; index directory already in use -> start refused\"},{\"id\":\"UC3\",\"actor\":\"System (OS)\",\"desc\":\"A reader process crashes or hangs.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Supervisor notices (process dead or no heartbeat) -> respawns the reader -> puts its unfinished papers back in line\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"One paper keeps crashing workers -> parked after N attempts, all other papers continue\"},{\"id\":\"UC4\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"The writer process crashes during ingestion.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Supervisor respawns the writer -> it recovers from its diary -> papers not yet acknowledged are sent again -> duplicates are ignored\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Too many restarts -> run stops with a clear error; the diary stays safe\"},{\"id\":\"UC5\",\"actor\":\"System (DBMS)\",\"desc\":\"Index is reopened after a crash or power cut.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Read MANIFEST -> delete segment folders it does not list -> replay diary entries after the last safe number -> start serving searches\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Diary tail is torn -> cut at last good record; MANIFEST damaged -> checksum error is reported instead of trusting it\"},{\"id\":\"UC6\",\"actor\":\"Researcher\",\"desc\":\"Researcher searches in natural language, with optional category and year filters.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Query analysed -> keyword and meaning searches run -> lists merged with RRF -> citation support reranks -> results shown with reasons\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No matches -> clear “no results” message; meaning model unavailable -> keyword-only results\"},{\"id\":\"UC7\",\"actor\":\"Researcher\",\"desc\":\"Researcher asks for key papers or papers like a chosen one.\",\"mainFlow\":\"System takes the paper or topic -> finds vector neighbours and runs personalized PageRank -> shows the list with reasons\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Paper has no citation data -> only text similarity is used\"},{\"id\":\"UC8\",\"actor\":\"Index Operator\",\"desc\":\"The citation graph is updated.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Fetch references from OpenAlex within the credit budget -> build graph -> compute PageRank and clusters -> publish the new graph version through the MANIFEST\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Credits used up -> stop, keep the old version, resume later\"},{\"id\":\"UC9\",\"actor\":\"System (DBMS)\",\"desc\":\"Small segments are merged in the background.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Choose small segments -> merge, drop replaced papers, rebuild vector structure -> publish by MANIFEST swap -> delete old segments when no query uses them\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Crash during merge -> unlisted new segment deleted at restart, old segments untouched\"},{\"id\":\"UC10\",\"actor\":\"Index Operator\",\"desc\":\"Operator checks index health.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Run fsck and stats -> every file checksum verified -> report of counts, segments and diary state\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Checksum mismatch -> the damaged file is named in the report and the index is not trusted until fixed\"},{\"id\":\"UC11\",\"actor\":\"Index Operator\",\"desc\":\"Operator runs the quality evaluation.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Load BEIR dataset and own queries -> index them -> run keyword, meaning, hybrid and reranked setups -> print table of nDCG, Recall, latency\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Dataset folder missing -> clear message with download steps\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR1\",\"criteria\":\"A 1,000-record run stores 1,000 valid records and resumes after a stop with no duplicates; arXiv requests are at least 3 s apart and a 503 with Retry-After is waited out.\",\"method\":\"tests/test_harvester.py; tests/test_coord.py; request-log check on a short live run\"},{\"req\":\"FR2\",\"criteria\":\"With readers killed at random and the writer killed twice, no paper is lost or duplicated; a paper that always crashes workers is parked; a second writer is refused.\",\"method\":\"tests/test_pipeline.py; tests/test_index.py\"},{\"req\":\"FR3\",\"criteria\":\"After a kill at each of 6 write steps and 12 random SIGKILLs every acknowledged paper is present; reopening removes incomplete segments and replays the diary; a merge does not change query results and a crash during a merge recovers.\",\"method\":\"tests/test_crash.py; tests/test_index.py; Phase 5 compaction tests (planned)\"},{\"req\":\"FR4\",\"criteria\":\"BM25 scores equal a brute-force implementation to 1e-9; compressed vector search recovers at least 95% of the exact top-10 with codes at most 1/8 of float32 size.\",\"method\":\"tests/test_tokenizer.py; tests/test_index.py; Phase 3 recall-versus-bits experiment (planned)\"},{\"req\":\"FR5\",\"criteria\":\"The hybrid list equals the reciprocal-rank fusion of the two lists; filters return only matching papers; PageRank matches a reference library within 1e-6; an ablation reports the citation rerank effect on nDCG@10.\",\"method\":\"Phase 3 to 5 tests and ablation (planned)\"},{\"req\":\"FR6\",\"criteria\":\"For 20 sampled results the matched words, meaning score and citation support are correct; MinHash estimates are within 0.05 of exact overlap; ingest, search, stats and fsck work from a clean checkout following the README.\",\"method\":\"Manual check plus CLI tests; Phase 4 tests (planned)\"},{\"req\":\"FR7\",\"criteria\":\"One command reproduces the table of nDCG@10, Recall@100, latency and size for every setup.\",\"method\":\"tools/eval_beir.py; metric unit tests in tests/test_index.py\"},{\"req\":\"FR8\",\"criteria\":\"The reranker can be switched on and off with an ablation; reranking 100 candidates takes at most 300 ms; “Key papers” lists the highest-PageRank papers of the topic.\",\"method\":\"Phase 6 ablation (planned)\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Figure: Complete Use Case Diagram — PaperLens Multi-Process Search Engine\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team04_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Clarify measurable NFR thresholds and link each acceptance criterion to an FR ID.",6,"{\"nfrTable\":\"Specify latency and throughput targets numerically.\"}","Ms. Umadevi F M","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B04-2","PRJ-B04",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B04-3","PRJ-B04",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/multi-process-search-engine\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B04-4","PRJ-B04",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B05-0","PRJ-B05",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Persistent Key-Value Storage Engine\",\"problem\":\"The proposed project is a Persistent Key-Value Storage System for storing and retrieving data using key-value pairs. Unlike temporary storage, the system aims to preserve stored data even after the application is closed or restarted. It provides a practical understanding of data storage, retrieval, and persistence.\",\"objectives\":[\"To design and implement suitable data structures for organizing and efficiently accessing key-value pairs.\",\"To develop a command interface with parsing and validation for handling basic key-value operations such as insertion, retrieval, updating, and deletion.\",\"To develop a persistent storage mechanism that preserves key-value data across application restarts.\",\"To implement mechanisms for maintaining data consistency and recovering from basic storage or application failures.\",\"To integrate the developed components into a functional key-value storage engine and validate its correctness and performance.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"File Management, Storage Management, Process Management\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Data Structures\",\"concepts\":\"Data Organization, Searching, Efficient Access\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Design and Analysis of Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Searching, Updating, Operation Efficiency, Complexity Analysis\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Database Management System\",\"concepts\":\"CRUD Operations\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Storing key-value data persistently\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Retrieving and accessing stored data\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Updating and managing existing key-value information\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Maintaining data availability and consistency during storage and retrieval\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Large-scale distributed storage\",\"reason\":\"Beyond the scope of a mini project\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Cloud-scale deployment and management\",\"reason\":\"Requires infrastructure beyond the current project scope\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Support for complex database operations\",\"reason\":\"The project focuses on the key-value storage model\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Aspect\",\"assessment\":\"Assessment\"},{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"The proposed system has a manageable scope for a 3-credit Mini Project and can be developed incrementally while studying the underlying storage and retrieval concepts.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"A standard computer, development environment, local storage, and basic testing resources are sufficient for the proposed project.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic knowledge of Data Structures, Operating Systems, OOP, Algorithms, and programming fundamentals is required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Limited development time and storage capacity.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Existing libraries and tools may be used for supporting or peripheral tasks, while the core storage and retrieval concepts will be implemented and studied by the team.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with scope modification.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Persistent Key-Value Storage Engine\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team05_conceptual_workflow.png?v=20261104\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Shrushti Gani","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B05-1","PRJ-B05",1,"Submitted","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall accept and validate custom NoSQL commands (SET, GET, UPDATE, DELETE) using the Parsing Engine.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Simple mechanism to manage and access key-value information (S0 Objective 3)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall create, update and delete key-value pairs using the SET, UPDATE and DELETE commands.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Store and update key-value data (S0 In-Scope 1, 3)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall retrieve the value associated with a key using the GET command.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Retrieve and access stored data (S0 In-Scope 2)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain an index mapping keys to storage locations for faster retrieval.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Efficient retrieval (S0 Objective 4)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall permanently store key-value records in local storage.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Store key-value data persistently (S0 In-Scope 1)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall recover stored data and rebuild the index when the application restarts.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Data availability and consistency (S0 In-Scope 4)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall detect invalid commands and display appropriate error messages.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Reliability and consistency (S0 Objective 4)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall report an error when a requested key does not exist.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Retrieve and access stored data (S0 In-Scope 2)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide a web interface for performing database operations and viewing results.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Simple mechanism to access stored information (S0 Objective 3)\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"The system shall identify and remove obsolete records through compaction to improve storage efficiency.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Limited storage capacity (S0 constraint)\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall respond to standard database operations within 2 seconds under normal workload.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"≤ 2 s per operation\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall retrieve a key-value record within 1 second for a database containing up to 10,000 records.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"≤ 1 s at 10,000 records\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support at least 10 concurrent user requests without crashing.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"≥ 10 concurrent requests; 0 crashes\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall keep all successfully stored records available after a normal restart.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"100% of stored records available after restart\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall recover stored data and rebuild the index within 10 seconds for up to 10,000 records.\",\"cat\":\"Recoverability\",\"target\":\"≤ 10 s at 10,000 records\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support at least 10,000 key-value records without requiring changes to the application architecture.\",\"cat\":\"Scalability\",\"target\":\"≥ 10,000 records; no architecture change\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Insert a key-value record (SET)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Enter SET command in web interface → parser validates (includes UC-09) → record stored and index updated → success shown within 2 s\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid command, empty key/value, or key already exists → error message within 1 s; stored data unchanged\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Retrieve a record using its key (GET)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Enter GET command → index gives record location → value read and shown within 1 s (up to 10,000 records)\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Key not found → \\\"Key not found\\\" error\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Update an existing record (UPDATE)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Enter UPDATE with key and new value → validated (includes UC-09) → new value stored → GET returns new value\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Key does not exist, or invalid value → error; old value unchanged\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Delete a record (DELETE)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Enter DELETE with key → validated (includes UC-09) → record removed → GET returns \\\"Key not found\\\"\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Key does not exist → error message\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"View all records\",\"mainFlow\":\"Open records view on web interface → all active key-value pairs listed (up to 10,000 records)\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No records stored → \\\"No records\\\" message\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Compact the database\",\"mainFlow\":\"Trigger compaction → obsolete records identified and removed → index rebuilt (includes UC-08) → storage size reduced; ≤ 30 s for 10,000 records\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Interruption or storage error → old storage kept, compaction aborted\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"Recover data after restart\",\"mainFlow\":\"Application restarts → stored records loaded → index rebuilt (includes UC-08) → system ready; ≤ 10 s for 10,000 records\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Storage file missing or last record incomplete → incomplete record skipped, error reported\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"Rebuild index (included use case)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Scan stored records → keep latest location for each key → index ready for lookup\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unreadable record → skipped and reported\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"Validate input (included use case)\",\"mainFlow\":\"Lexical check → syntax check → semantic check → structured instruction produced within 100 ms\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unknown command, missing key or bad syntax → command rejected with error\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"A valid SET, GET, UPDATE or DELETE command is accepted and converted into a structured instruction in ≤ 100 ms.\",\"method\":\"Parser test with at least 10 valid commands of each type; measure parse time\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"SET adds a new record; UPDATE changes the value returned for that key; DELETE makes the key return \\\"Key not found\\\". SET on a key that already exists is rejected with an error and the old value is kept. Each operation completes in ≤ 2 s.\",\"method\":\"SET → GET, UPDATE → GET, DELETE → GET, duplicate SET; measure time\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"GET returns the current value of an existing key in ≤ 1 s with up to 10,000 records stored.\",\"method\":\"GET test on datasets of 100, 1,000 and 10,000 records; record latency\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Every stored key has exactly one index entry pointing to its current record; index lookup takes ≤ 10 ms.\",\"method\":\"Index check after SET / UPDATE / DELETE; timing test on 10,000 keys\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Every successfully written record is present in local storage and can be read back (100%).\",\"method\":\"Store → close application → reopen → retrieve\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"After restart, all stored records are readable and the index is rebuilt in ≤ 10 s for 10,000 records.\",\"method\":\"Restart test with a 10,000-record dataset; timed\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Unknown commands, missing keys or wrong syntax are rejected with a clear error message within 1 s, and no stored data changes.\",\"method\":\"Invalid-command test with at least 10 cases\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"GET, UPDATE or DELETE on a missing key returns \\\"Key not found\\\" within 1 s.\",\"method\":\"Missing-key test for each command\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"All four commands can be run and their results viewed in the web interface, and all stored records can be listed (UC-05); each result appears within 2 s of submission.\",\"method\":\"UI functional test of every command; measure response time\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-10\",\"criteria\":\"After compaction, 100% of superseded and deleted records are removed, storage size is reduced, and every current value is still retrievable. Compaction takes ≤ 30 s for 10,000 records.\",\"method\":\"Many UPDATE / DELETE operations → compact → compare file size before and after → GET all keys\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Each standard operation responds in ≤ 2 s under normal workload (one user, up to 10,000 records).\",\"method\":\"Timed run of SET, GET, UPDATE and DELETE; report maximum and average\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"GET for a record completes in ≤ 1 s with 10,000 records stored.\",\"method\":\"100 GET requests on a 10,000-record dataset; report maximum and average\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"At least 10 requests sent at the same time are all handled with no crash and no corrupted record.\",\"method\":\"Concurrency script with 10 parallel requests; check results and stored data\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"100% of records stored successfully are still available after a normal restart.\",\"method\":\"Store 1,000 records → restart → compare all records\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Recovery of stored data and index rebuild finish in ≤ 10 s for up to 10,000 records.\",\"method\":\"Timed restart with 10,000 records\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"10,000 records can be stored and used with no change to the application architecture, and NFR ID-01 / NFR ID-02 still hold.\",\"method\":\"Load 10,000 records; rerun operation timing tests\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team05_use_case_diagram.png?v=20261104\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B05-2","PRJ-B05",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B05-3","PRJ-B05",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/persistent-key-value-engine\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B05-4","PRJ-B05",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A17-0","PRJ-A17",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Distributed File Metadata & Access Management Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A17-1","PRJ-A17",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A17-2","PRJ-A17",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A17-3","PRJ-A17",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A17-4","PRJ-A17",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A09-0","PRJ-A09",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Multi-Source Data Integration & Entity Resolution Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A09-1","PRJ-A09",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A09-2","PRJ-A09",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A09-3","PRJ-A09",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A09-4","PRJ-A09",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B10-0","PRJ-B10",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Large-Scale Log Processing & Analytics Engine\",\"problem\":\"Modern applications and servers generate log files that grow to many gigabytes. Each line is unstructured text, and the files often contain malformed lines and repeated entries. Because of this, the logs cannot be searched or analysed directly, and loading such a file fully into memory is not practical on an ordinary machine.\\n\\nThis project builds a single-machine pipeline that reads one massive log file efficiently, converts raw lines into structured records, removes bad and duplicate data, stores the clean records in a database and presents analytics on a web dashboard.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a very large single log file in newline-aligned chunks using memory mapping, without loading the whole file into RAM.\",\"Parse each raw line into structured fields (timestamp, level, IP address, error code, message) and validate them, sending malformed lines to a quarantine store with a reason.\",\"Remove duplicate records with a bounded sliding-window fingerprint set so that memory use stays flat.\",\"Store clean records in PostgreSQL through in-memory micro-batches and bulk inserts, with retry on failed writes.\",\"Provide aggregate analytics (counts by level, error code and time) through a REST API with time-range and log-level filters, shown on a live web dashboard.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":1,\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Memory-mapped files (mmap), virtual memory and page cache, file descriptors, system calls, chunked I/O\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":2,\"course\":\"Data Structures & Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Regular-expression parsing, hashing and fingerprints, hash sets, queues, sliding-window eviction, complexity analysis\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":3,\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Relational schema design, bulk insert, transactions, indexing, aggregate SQL, query tuning\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":4,\"course\":\"Web Technologies\",\"concepts\":\"REST API design, HTTP and JSON, client-side charts, filtering through query parameters\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[{\"id\":\"E1\",\"name\":\"Chunked Ingestion Engine\",\"desc\":\"Memory-mapped, chunked reading of a single large log file on one machine without loading the full file into RAM.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"name\":\"Parsing & Quarantine Engine\",\"desc\":\"Regex-based parsing into structured fields, validation, and quarantine of malformed lines with line number and reason.\"},{\"id\":\"E3\",\"name\":\"De-duplication & Batching Engine\",\"desc\":\"Bounded sliding-window duplicate removal using fingerprints, a hash set and a queue, followed by micro-batch buffering.\"},{\"id\":\"E4\",\"name\":\"Storage & Analytics Engine\",\"desc\":\"Bulk storage in PostgreSQL, aggregate SQL analytics, and a REST API with a web dashboard showing live charts with time-range and log-level filters.\"}],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"sl\":1,\"item\":\"Memory-mapped, chunked reading of a single large log file on one machine (E1 Ingestion Engine).\"},{\"sl\":2,\"item\":\"Regex-based parsing into structured fields, validation, and quarantine of malformed lines with line number and reason.\"},{\"sl\":3,\"item\":\"Bounded sliding-window duplicate removal using fingerprints, a hash set and a queue, followed by micro-batch buffering.\"},{\"sl\":4,\"item\":\"Bulk storage in PostgreSQL, aggregate SQL analytics, and a REST API with a web dashboard showing live charts with time-range and log-level filters.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"sl\":1,\"item\":\"Multi-source, distributed or real-time streaming ingestion\",\"reason\":\"The project is scoped to one log file on one machine; distributed systems would be complex.\"},{\"sl\":2,\"item\":\"Machine-learning anomaly detection, alerting and log-based security analytics\",\"reason\":\"Analytics are limited to aggregate counts and trends; advanced analytics is a separate research area.\"},{\"sl\":3,\"item\":\"User authentication, multi-user access control and production deployment or cloud hosting\",\"reason\":\"Not part of the core mechanisms being studied; the dashboard runs locally for demonstration.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"detail\":\"Achievable as a 3-credit mini project. The pipeline has four small, well-defined engines (ingestion, parsing and de-duplication, storage, dashboard) that can be split across four team members and integrated step by step.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"detail\":\"One laptop or desktop (8 cores, 16 GB RAM), a multi-GB sample log file, PostgreSQL, and a web browser. No special hardware or cloud service is needed.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"detail\":\"Operating systems (memory mapping, file I/O), data structures (hashing, queues, regex), PostgreSQL, REST/HTTP basics, and a programming language such as C++.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"detail\":\"Single machine and single log source only; limited by 16 GB RAM, so memory must stay bounded; duplicates are detected only within a fixed window; throughput is limited by disk speed and database write speed.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"detail\":\"Libraries are used only for peripheral work: the regex module, a PostgreSQL driver, a web framework and a charting library. The core mechanisms (chunked mmap reading, parsing, windowed de-duplication, micro-batching and aggregate queries) are implemented by the team.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"detail\":\"High. The scope is bounded, the technology is familiar from the foundation courses, and every part can be tested with measurable targets.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Single-Machine Log Processing Pipeline Conceptual Workflow\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team_b10_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Umadevi F M","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B10-1","PRJ-B10",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall open the single log file read-only and read it through memory mapping (mmap) in newline-aligned chunks of configurable size (default 64 MB), without loading the whole file into RAM.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"Handle a massive single log file on one machine\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall parse every raw log line with a regular expression into timestamp, level, IP address, error code and message.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"Turn raw text logs into structured fields\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall validate parsed fields and send malformed or incomplete lines to a quarantine file with line number and reason.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"Bad data must not crash the pipeline or vanish silently\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall drop duplicate records by computing a fingerprint of the key fields and checking it against a bounded hash set.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"Avoid duplicate entries inflating counts, with flat memory\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall hold clean records in an in-memory queue and flush them as one micro-batch when the batch-size limit is reached and stored in PostgreSQL.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"Efficient writes instead of row-by-row inserts and persistent, consistent storage\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall expose the analytics through a REST API returning JSON, with aggregate SQL analytics time-range and log-level filters and display them on a web dashboard.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"source\":\"View results in a browser\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Ingestion-to-storage throughput on a single machine.\",\"category\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"At least 1000 lines/sec end to end on a 4-core, 8 GB machine with a 5 GB test file.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Aggregate analytics queries must be fast on large tables.\",\"category\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Each dashboard query completes in under 1 second on a table of 1000 rows.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"A bad line or a failed batch write must not stop the pipeline.\",\"category\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"0 crashes in fault-injection tests; a failed batch is retried up to 3 times before being logged.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Every input line must be accounted for and no duplicate inside the window may be stored.\",\"category\":\"Data integrity\",\"target\":\"lines read = valid + duplicate + quarantined for every run; 0 in-window duplicates in the database.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"System Operator\",\"story\":\"As an operator, I want to start an ingestion run for a log file with chosen settings so that the file is processed into the database.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Operator gives file path, chunk size, window size N and batch size. 2. System checks the file can be opened. 3. System starts the run: the file is read, parsed, de-duplicated and stored.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"Empty file: the run completes with zero records. File smaller than one chunk: processed as a single chunk.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"File missing or unreadable: show error, no run is created.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"System Operator\",\"story\":\"As an operator, I want each line parsed and validated so that only structured, correct records move forward.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Match the line against the regex. 2. Extract timestamp, level, IP address, error code and message. 3. Validate the fields. 4. Pass the valid record to duplicate removal.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"A line with an optional field missing: fallback value used where one is defined.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Line does not match or has missing required fields: write it to the quarantine file with line number and reason, then continue.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"System Operator\",\"story\":\"As the operator, I want duplicate records removed so that the counts are not inflated.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Compute the fingerprint of the key fields. 2. If it is not in the hash set, add it, push it to the window queue and pass the record on. 3. If the window already holds N fingerprints, evict the oldest first.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"A repeat that arrives after more than N records: accepted as new. Window not yet full: nothing is evicted.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Duplicate found: drop the record and count it as a duplicate.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"System Operator\",\"story\":\"As an operator, I want to review the quarantined lines so that I can see which log lines were rejected and why.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Operator opens the quarantine file after a run. 2. System lists each rejected line with its reason. 3. Operator checks the reasons.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"Quarantine file is empty: show 'no rejected lines'.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Quarantine file missing or unreadable: show an error message.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"System Operator\",\"story\":\"As the operator, I want clean records saved in micro-batches so that the database is written to efficiently.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Records collect in the in-memory queue. 2. When the batch-size limit is reached, bulk insert the batch into PostgreSQL. 3. Commit and record the batch statistics. 4. Clear the queue.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"Final batch smaller than the limit: flushed at the end of the run.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Database unavailable or the write fails: keep the batch, retry up to 3 times, then log the error and stop cleanly.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"SOC Analyst\",\"story\":\"As an analyst, I want to see error trends and event frequency so that I can understand what is happening in the logs.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Open dashboard. 2. Dashboard calls the REST API. 3. System runs aggregate SQL. 4. Charts are drawn from the JSON response.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"Only one record stored: the charts show a single point.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No data for the period: show 'no data' message. Database unreachable: show error, keep last chart.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"SOC Analyst\",\"story\":\"As an analyst, I want to filter by time range and log level so that I can focus on the data I care about.\",\"mainFlow\":\"1. Analyst selects time range and level. 2. Dashboard sends them as query parameters. 3. Charts refresh with the filtered results.\",\"boundaryFlow\":\"Range covers a single minute or a single log level: the charts show only that slice.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid or reversed range: API returns HTTP 400 with a JSON message and the charts stay unchanged.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"For a file larger than RAM the engine maps it read-only and yields chunks of the configured size; every chunk ends on a newline; joined chunks are byte-identical to the source file.\",\"method\":\"Unit test with mock offsets; checksum comparison of joined chunks with the original file.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"For a sample of 1,000 well-formed lines, 100% are parsed with correct timestamp, level, IP, error code and message.\",\"method\":\"Unit test comparing parsed fields with the labelled expected values.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Every injected malformed or incomplete line appears in the quarantine file with line number and reason.\",\"method\":\"Test file with known bad lines; count quarantine entries and database rows.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Duplicates inside the window are dropped; assert set size <= N.\",\"method\":\"Unit tests: assert set size <= N.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"A batch is flushed when it reaches the batch-size limit; stored row count equals the valid unique records sent.\",\"method\":\"Unit test with a synthetic stream; bulk-insert test compared with SELECT COUNT(*).\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"Aggregate queries return correct counts by level, error code and minute on a dataset; the endpoint returns valid JSON with HTTP 200 and HTTP 400 for invalid ones; the dashboard draws charts from the response.\",\"method\":\"Run SQL on dataset and compare with expected values; API test (Postman); manual dashboard check.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Throughput of at least 1000 lines/sec on a 4-core, 8 GB machine with a 5 GB file.\",\"method\":\"Timed run: lines read divided by elapsed time.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Each dashboard aggregate query finishes in under 1 second on 1000 rows.\",\"method\":\"EXPLAIN ANALYZE and timed runs, repeated 10 times.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Bad lines and a stopped database cause no crash; a failed batch is retried up to 3 times and then logged.\",\"method\":\"Fault injection: malformed input and database shutdown during a run.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"For every run lines read = valid + duplicate + quarantined, and no in-window duplicate fingerprint exists in the database.\",\"method\":\"Compare run counters; SQL query for repeated fingerprints.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram - Large-Scale Log Processing & Analytics Engine\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team_b10_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Umadevi F M","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B10-2","PRJ-B10",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B10-3","PRJ-B10",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/log-analytics-engine\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B10-4","PRJ-B10",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A08-0","PRJ-A08",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Large-Scale Dataset Profiling & Data Quality Engine\",\"problem\":\"Structured datasets can contain missing values, duplicate records, inconsistent types, invalid values, and statistically unusual observations. The proposed project brings dataset profiling and data-quality assessment into a single workflow so users can inspect a dataset, identify quality issues, select supported corrections, and revalidate the results. The presentations describe an upload-to-report workflow and emphasize preserving the original dataset while applying corrections to a separate copy.\",\"objectives\":[\"To accept supported structured/tabular datasets and generate a structural profile.\",\"To identify missing values, duplicate rows, data-type inconsistencies, and rule violations.\",\"To compute descriptive statistics and flag potentially unusual observations.\",\"To allow users to review detected issues and apply selected supported corrections without changing the original dataset.\",\"To revalidate corrected data and present data-quality results and reports.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Dataset structure, schema, data types, consistency, storage and retrieval\",\"role\":\"Dominant (inferred from presentation emphasis)\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Data Structures & Algorithm Analysis\",\"concepts\":\"Scanning, comparison/hashing for duplicate detection, efficient data processing\",\"role\":\"Dominant (inferred)\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Probability & Statistics\",\"concepts\":\"Descriptive statistics, distributions, missing-value rates, IQR-based outlier screening\",\"role\":\"Dominant (inferred)\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Machine Learning\",\"concepts\":\"Anomaly-analysis concepts and interpretation of unusual observations\",\"role\":\"Supporting (inferred)\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Files, memory, processing and resource limits\",\"role\":\"Supporting (inferred)\"},{\"sl\":\"6\",\"course\":\"Web Technologies\",\"concepts\":\"Dataset upload, dashboard, user interactions and reporting\",\"role\":\"Supporting (inferred)\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Upload and inspect supported structured/tabular datasets.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Profile datasets and detect missing, duplicate, inconsistent and invalid values.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Perform basic statistical analysis and flag possible outliers.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Display findings, allow selected supported corrections, revalidate, and produce quality results.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Enterprise-scale deployment\",\"reason\":\"Beyond initial student project and resource scope\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Real-time enterprise monitoring\",\"reason\":\"Excluded from initial integration scope\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Support for every database/cloud platform or input format\",\"reason\":\"Limited supported structured/tabular formats and integrations\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"A 15-week semester and limited student development resources are identified; a formal 3-credit feasibility conclusion is not provided.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"Dataset processing requires local file handling, memory and storage; exact equipment specifications are not stated.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"DBMS, DAA, Probability & Statistics, Machine Learning concepts, OS and Web Technologies.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Limited supported input formats; memory/storage demands for larger datasets; supported corrections only; original data must remain unchanged.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Not explicitly specified in the slides.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Not explicitly assessed in the slides; requires faculty confirmation.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Large-Scale Dataset Profiling & Data Quality Engine\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team08_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Sneha Varur","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A08-1","PRJ-A08",1,"Revision requested","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall allow users to upload datasets in supported structured/tabular formats and ingest the data for analysis.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Automated profiling\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall detect and report common data-quality issues including missing values, duplicate records, data-type inconsistencies, null values and basic invalid values.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Automated detection of common data-quality issues\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall analyze the dataset using basic statistics, such as average, minimum, maximum, and distribution, and identify unusual values (outliers).\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Unified quality analysis\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall display the detected issues and allow the user to select which issues should be corrected.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Automated detection of common data-quality issues\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall automatically apply the user-selected corrections, such as correcting supported data types and removing duplicate records, without modifying the original dataset.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Basic recommendations for detected issues\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall recheck the corrected dataset and allow the user to perform additional correction rounds until the user chooses to finish and allow to generate quality report.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Unified quality analysis\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide basic recommendations for suitable corrective actions for detected quality issues.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Basic recommendations for detected issues\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support efficient processing of larger datasets within the defined system resource limits.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Scalable processing of large datasets\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall process datasets within the supported size limit without failure under the defined test environment.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Successfully process ≥95% of defined test datasets within the supported size limit\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system should quickly show the user the progress or result while the dataset is being uploaded and analyzed.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Display processing status within 2 seconds of starting a long-running operation\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall preserve the original uploaded dataset without modifying it during quality analysis.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability / Data Integrity\",\"target\":\"0 unintended modifications to original input files in test cases\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide understandable quality results to users through the dashboard.\",\"cat\":\"Usability\",\"target\":\"A user shall be able to identify the overall score and major detected issues within 1 minute of viewing the results\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide consistent results when the same dataset and rules are processed repeatedly.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"Identical input and configuration shall produce identical results in repeated tests\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall prevent unauthorized access to uploaded datasets and generated results.\",\"cat\":\"Security\",\"target\":\"Unauthorized access attempts are rejected in all security tests\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain correction results so that users can distinguish the original and corrected datasets.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"100% of correction sessions preserve original/corrected distinction\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants to upload a supported dataset for quality analysis.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select file → Upload → System validates file → Dataset accepted.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unsupported/corrupted/empty file → System displays error and rejects the file.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants to view the structure and basic characteristics of the uploaded dataset\",\"mainFlow\":\"System reads dataset → identifies rows, columns, data types and basic statistics → Displays profile.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Parsing/type problem → System flags the affected data and continues where possible.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants to identify problems in the dataset.\",\"mainFlow\":\"System checks missing values, duplicates, invalid values, data-type inconsistencies and outliers → Displays detected issues.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"A rule cannot be applied → System marks the issue for review.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants to choose which detected problems should be corrected.\",\"mainFlow\":\"User reviews issues → Selects corrections → Confirms → System applies selected corrections to a separate copy.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Correction is not applicable/fails → System reports the problem and keeps the affected data unchanged\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants to check the corrected dataset again.\",\"mainFlow\":\"System re-profiles corrected dataset → Runs quality checks → Displays updated results.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"New or remaining problems found → System shows them for another correction round.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"User wants the final quality of the corrected dataset.\",\"mainFlow\":\"User chooses finish → System performs final analysis → Calculates quality score → Generates dashboard and downloadable report.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Report generation fails → System displays error and allows retry.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"Admin\",\"desc\":\"Admin wants to manage the rules used for quality checking.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Admin adds/updates/deactivates rules → System validates the rule → Saves the updated rule set.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid/conflicting rule → System rejects the rule and shows the reason.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"Admin\",\"desc\":\"Admin wants to control which file formats can be uploaded\",\"mainFlow\":\"Admin adds/enables/disables supported formats → System updates the allowed format list.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unsupported or invalid configuration → System rejects the change.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"Admin\",\"desc\":\"Admin wants to monitor processing and system activity.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Admin views processing status, errors and activity information → System displays monitoring information.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Monitoring data unavailable → System shows an appropriate error/status message.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"The system accepts supported dataset files and successfully loads valid files for processing. Unsupported, empty, or corrupted files are rejected with an error message.\",\"method\":\"Upload valid and invalid test files and verify the result.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"The system displays the dataset row count, column count, column names, data types, and basic profile information correctly.\",\"method\":\"Compare displayed profile results with the known dataset values.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"The system correctly detects and reports missing values, duplicate records, data-type inconsistencies, and defined invalid values.\",\"method\":\"Test datasets containing known quality issues and compare detected results with expected results.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"The system calculates the defined statistical measures, identifies applicable outliers, and displays the results correctly.\",\"method\":\"Run the system on test datasets and compare results with independently calculated values.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"The system generates a quality score based on the defined scoring rules and displays the score correctly.\",\"method\":\"Use datasets with known quality issues and verify the calculated score against the defined formula.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"The system displays the detected quality results, final quality score and analysis through the dashboard and generates a downloadable quality report.\",\"method\":\"Complete the analysis and verify dashboard values and downloaded report values.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"The system provides a correction recommendation for supported detected quality issues and the recommendation is relevant to the detected issue.\",\"method\":\"Create datasets with supported issues and verify the displayed recommendations.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"The system processes datasets within the defined supported size/resource limit without failure and completes the quality-analysis workflow.\",\"method\":\"Test datasets of increasing sizes and measure successful completion and processing time.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team08_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Clarify measurable NFR thresholds and link each acceptance criterion to an FR ID.",6,"{\"nfrTable\":\"Specify latency and throughput targets numerically.\"}","Ms. Sneha Varur","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A08-2","PRJ-A08",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A08-3","PRJ-A08",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/data-quality-engine\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A08-4","PRJ-A08",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A13-0","PRJ-A13",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Distributed Event Processing & Notification Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A13-1","PRJ-A13",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A13-2","PRJ-A13",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A13-3","PRJ-A13",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A13-4","PRJ-A13",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A10-0","PRJ-A10",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Dependency-Aware Parallel Build System\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A10-1","PRJ-A10",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A10-2","PRJ-A10",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A10-3","PRJ-A10",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A10-4","PRJ-A10",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A14-0","PRJ-A14",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Mini-kernel with process, memory and file management\",\"problem\":\"Our project focuses on understanding how a computer can start and run its own basic operating-system kernel. A kernel is the core part of an operating system that manages access to hardware resources and provides essential services to programs. Developing even a small kernel involves several connected responsibilities, such as starting execution after boot, managing memory, handling processes, and organizing data on storage.\\n\\nOur proposed mini-kernel is a small x86-64 operating-system kernel developed from the ground up for learning and demonstration. It will be loaded by a bootloader and executed in an emulated computer environment during development. The project aims to implement selected kernel-level facilities and demonstrate how they work together. The work will use xv6 and other educational resources only for understanding operating-system concepts; the xv6 codebase will not be used as the project foundation.\\n\\nWe consider this a suitable Mini Project because it gives us an opportunity to study operating-system fundamentals through implementation rather than only theoretical discussion. A controlled scope will allow us to demonstrate the basic working of a kernel without attempting to build a complete commercial operating system.\",\"objectives\":[\"To understand the role of a kernel in an operating system and study the basic stages involved in starting kernel execution.\",\"To design and develop a small x86-64 kernel from scratch, using an existing bootloader as the initial loading mechanism.\",\"To explore and implement selected fundamental kernel responsibilities related to process, memory and file management.\",\"To demonstrate the kernel in an emulated hardware environment and observe its behaviour during execution.\",\"To apply foundational Computer Science and system-software concepts through a practical, demonstrable operating-system project.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Operating System Principles\",\"concepts\":\"Kernel responsibilities, process management, memory management, file systems, interrupts and resource management\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Computer Organization and Architecture\",\"concepts\":\"Processor basics, boot process, CPU operation, memory hierarchy and hardware interaction\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Data Structures & Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Queues, lists, trees or other suitable structures, searching, scheduling concepts and efficient management of kernel data\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Study and demonstrate the boot flow in which a bootloader loads the mini-kernel and transfers control to it.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Develop a small kernel that can initialize and execute in a controlled emulated environment.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Explore and implement basic process-management concepts, within the limits of the project scope.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Explore and implement basic memory-management and file-management facilities, including a demonstration of persistent data using an emulated storage image if feasible.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"A complete general-purpose operating system with full POSIX or Linux compatibility\",\"reason\":\"This would require a much larger development effort and is beyond the time and credit limits of the Mini Project.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Support for multiple processor architectures\",\"reason\":\"The project is restricted to keep implementation and testing manageable.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Graphical desktop environment, networking stack and broad peripheral support\",\"reason\":\"These features add substantial complexity and are not required to demonstrate the selected kernel fundamentals.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Technical Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The x86-64 architecture, C programming, assembly language, and an existing bootloader provide a suitable foundation for developing the mini-kernel.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Software Resource Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Required open-source tools such as GCC, NASM, Make, Git, and QEMU are available for kernel development and testing.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Hardware Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"A standard x86-64 laptop with WSL Ubuntu is sufficient to develop and test the kernel using QEMU emulation.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Knowledge Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Concepts from Operating Systems, Computer Organization and Architecture, C programming, and Data Structures support the development process.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Time and Team Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The project can be developed incrementally by dividing tasks among four team members and focusing on the selected Process, Memory, and File Management functionalities.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major Constraints & Limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Time limitation within academic duration; hardware testing restricted to QEMU emulation; low-level technical complexity of CPU/memory interfaces; kernel debugging challenges; focused bounded scope rather than a commercial operating system.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini-kernel with process, memory and file management\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team14_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Arundati Aralimatti","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A14-1","PRJ-A14",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Boot and initialize the educational mini-kernel in a controlled emulated environment and make kernel output available.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Create, initialize, track and terminate bounded kernel processes using process identity and lifecycle states.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Allocate and release fixed-size memory pages while maintaining page-availability state.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Select an eligible process and perform a CPU context transfer between process contexts.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Create, open, read, write, close and remove files using bounded kernel file facilities.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Associate file resources with processes through process identity and file descriptors.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"Dispatch supported process, memory and file service requests through the kernel-service interface.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N2\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"Record selected process, memory, file and service events with information needed to inspect resource-state changes.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N3\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"Measure selected execution/resource operations and make performance evidence available for analysis.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N4\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"Demonstrate and validate an integrated Process–Memory–File interaction within the kernel.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0-N5\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Invalid or unsafe page requests shall be rejected without corrupting allocator state.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"No invalid/double-free test may alter valid allocation state.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The kernel shall execute process, memory and file management operations within specified CPU cycle limits.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Each supported operation shall complete within its experimentally established CPU cycle threshold under identical QEMU test conditions.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Process, Memory and File responsibilities shall retain clear ownership boundaries.\",\"cat\":\"Modularity\",\"target\":\"Engines use defined interfaces and do not directly corrupt another engine's internal state.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Validation shall be reproducible in the documented emulated environment.\",\"cat\":\"Reproducibility\",\"target\":\"Documented build/run/test procedure reproduces the validation sequence.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Observation and performance outputs shall be understandable to learners.\",\"cat\":\"Educational usability\",\"target\":\"Each demonstration identifies operation, result and relevant state/event/measurement.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The Round Robin scheduler shall ensure fair CPU allocation among ready processes using a fixed time quantum.\",\"cat\":\"Scheduling Fairness\",\"target\":\"Every continuously ready process shall receive CPU time in cyclic order, with no process being indefinitely starved.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Start the mini-kernel in the controlled environment.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Start kernel → initialize required resources → kernel becomes ready → display startup status.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Kernel fails to initialize → display failure information → halt safely.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Create a process for an executable workload.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Submit workload → create process → assign process identity → set initial execution state → process becomes ready.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid workload or process-creation failure → report failure → do not create an invalid process.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Observe and manage the execution state of an active process.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process enters a state → state changes during execution → current state is maintained → state change is recorded.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid state transition → reject transition → retain valid process state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Terminate a process and release its associated resources.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process completes/termination requested → terminate process → release associated resources → record termination.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Termination/resource-release failure → report failure → preserve valid resource state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Allocate memory for process execution.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process requests memory → check available memory → allocate required memory → update memory state.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Insufficient memory → allocation fails → report memory-allocation failure without corrupting state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Release memory associated with a terminated process.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process terminates → identify allocated memory → release memory → update available-memory state.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Memory release inconsistency → report error → retain resource information for validation.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Perform basic file creation or opening.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process requests file operation → create/open file → update file state → return operation result.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid/unavailable file operation → return failure → maintain valid file state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Perform basic file read/write operations.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Process requests read/write → perform operation → update relevant file state → return result.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid operation or unavailable file → report failure → preserve valid file state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Observe selected kernel events and resource-state changes during execution.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Kernel activity occurs → record selected event → capture relevant resource state → associate event and state → inspect event sequence and available evidence.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Event/state information unavailable or inconsistent → record incomplete observation → identify observation gap → continue without corrupting kernel state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-10\",\"actor\":\"Learner / User\",\"desc\":\"Measure performance of a controlled workload and repeat it for validation.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Run workload → collect defined performance metrics → record measurements → repeat same workload under same conditions → compare results.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Measurement unavailable, inconsistent, or varies beyond defined conditions → mark measurement invalid → repeat controlled validation run.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Kernel starts successfully in the controlled environment and reaches the required startup stage.\",\"method\":\"Kernel startup test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"A process is created with a valid identity and state, and can be terminated correctly.\",\"method\":\"Process lifecycle test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Memory pages can be allocated and released while maintaining correct memory state.\",\"method\":\"Memory allocation/release test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"A READY process is selected and its context is switched correctly.\",\"method\":\"Scheduler/context-switch test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Files can be created, opened, read, written, closed and removed correctly.\",\"method\":\"File operation test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"A valid PID/FD identifies the intended file resource.\",\"method\":\"Process-file integration test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Supported service requests reach the correct subsystem and unsupported requests are rejected.\",\"method\":\"Service dispatch test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Selected process, memory and file operations generate the required event records.\",\"method\":\"Event recording test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"Selected operations produce measurable performance results using the defined measurement method.\",\"method\":\"Performance measurement test\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-10\",\"criteria\":\"At least one Process–Memory–File interaction completes successfully and its result/events can be observed.\",\"method\":\"Integrated validation test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Invalid or unsafe memory requests are rejected without changing valid memory state.\",\"method\":\"Invalid allocation/free test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"When a resource limit is reached, the system reports failure without overwriting valid entries.\",\"method\":\"Resource exhaustion test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Recorded events contain the required information consistently.\",\"method\":\"Event record inspection\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Performance measurements are obtained using the same defined procedure and test conditions, producing comparable cycle-based evidence\",\"method\":\"Repeated performance measurements\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Process, Memory and File components use their defined responsibilities without directly corrupting another component's state.\",\"method\":\"Interface/integration test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"The documented build, run and test steps can be repeated in the controlled environment.\",\"method\":\"Repeat build/run/test\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Demonstrations clearly show the operation, result and relevant event/state/measurement information.\",\"method\":\"Evidence inspection\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team14_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Arundati Aralimatti","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A14-2","PRJ-A14",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A14-3","PRJ-A14",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/mini-kernel-os\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A14-4","PRJ-A14",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B07-0","PRJ-B07",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine\",\"problem\":\"Java source programs pass through several compilation stages before they can be executed by the Java Virtual Machine. These stages include lexical analysis, syntax analysis, semantic checking, intermediate representation generation, code optimization and bytecode generation. Studying these stages separately can make it difficult to understand how they work together in a complete compiler.\\n\\nThe implemented project is a Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine for a defined Java subset. It uses a Flex-based lexer and a Bison parser based on a 53-rule grammar to process source programs and construct an Abstract Syntax Tree. The compiler performs semantic analysis using scoped symbol information, generates Three-Address Code (TAC), applies intermediate-code optimization passes, and uses a Java ASM backend to generate JVM class files. The C++ front end exports the optimized, type-annotated program as a typed intermediate representation (JSON), which the Java ASM backend reads to produce the class file. The generated class is then executed on the JVM, so the complete flow is organised as seven phases: scanner, parser, semantic analysis, TAC generation, optimization, bytecode generation and execution.\\n\\nThe project also provides a web-based interface for demonstrating the compilation phases and viewing their intermediate results, optimization information, generated bytecode and execution output. The implementation is limited to the language constructs supported by its grammar and backend; it is not a complete Java compiler.\",\"objectives\":[\"To develop an integrated compiler pipeline that processes programs written using the supported Java language subset.\",\"To implement lexical analysis, grammar-based syntax analysis, AST construction and semantic checking.\",\"To generate Three-Address Code and apply intermediate-representation optimization techniques.\",\"To generate JVM bytecode using the Java ASM backend, execute the generated class using the existing JVM and inspect the program output.\",\"To provide a web-based demonstration interface through which users can inspect the major compilation phases and their results.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Principles of Compiler Design\",\"concepts\":\"Regular expressions and lexical patterns; tokens; Context-Free Grammar (CFG); BNF productions; Flex-based lexical analysis; Bison LALR(1) parsing; syntax-directed AST construction; semantic analysis; intermediate-code and bytecode generation.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Data Structures and Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Abstract Syntax Trees and tree traversal; scoped symbol tables and hash-based lookup; stacks for parser/scope processing; vectors and maps for tokens and IR; Three-Address Code; control-flow labels; optimization passes including constant folding, constant propagation, copy propagation, common subexpression elimination and dead-code/branch elimination.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Object-Oriented Programming\",\"concepts\":\"C++ classes and objects; structures and enumerations; inheritance and polymorphism where used in AST/compiler components; modular design and encapsulation.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Java, JVM and Web Technology\",\"concepts\":\"Java-based ASM backend; JVM class files, bytecode, operand stack and local variables; HTML, CSS and JavaScript interface; Python standard-library web server for displaying compilation phases and results.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Flex-based lexical analysis and Bison-based syntax analysis using the project’s BNF grammar.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"AST construction, scoped symbol-table management and semantic/type checking for supported constructs.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Three-Address Code generation, intermediate-representation optimization and JVM bytecode generation using the Java ASM backend.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Execution using the existing JVM and a web-based interface for viewing compilation phases, optimization information and program output.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"A complete implementation of the Java language and full Java language compatibility.\",\"reason\":\"The project implements a defined subset; complete Java semantics and all language features are beyond the mini-project scope.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Full support for advanced features such as generics, lambdas, concurrency, exception handling and the complete Java type system.\",\"reason\":\"These features require additional parsing, semantic analysis and JVM code-generation work. Grammar recognition or sample presence alone does not imply complete end-to-end support.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Production-grade compiler optimization, native machine-code generation, a full-featured IDE and cloud-scale compilation.\",\"reason\":\"The project focuses on an educational compiler pipeline, selected IR optimization passes and JVM bytecode generation.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible as a mini-project when restricted to the language constructs implemented by the lexer, parser, semantic analyzer and backend.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"A Windows computer with a C++17 compiler (g++/MinGW), Flex and Bison (bundled in the project), Java JDK 17 or newer (java, javac and javap on PATH), the ASM 9.8 library (lib/asm.jar) and Python 3.9 or newer.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Compiler design, regular expressions, CFG/BNF, parsing, data structures and algorithms, C++ programming, Java and JVM fundamentals.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"The compiler supports a defined subset rather than full Java. Some constructs may be recognized by the grammar without complete backend/runtime support; end-to-end support must be validated with test programs. Inheritance is limited to single-class extends (interfaces are not supported), and the provided build and run scripts (javacc.exe, .bat files) target Windows.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Flex is used for lexical analysis and Bison for grammar-based parsing and AST construction. C++ components perform semantic analysis, TAC generation and optimization. A Java ASM backend generates JVM class files and computes stack-map frames, while expression lowering, control-flow lowering and descriptor selection are implemented in the project’s own backend code. • The class files are executed by the existing JVM. A Python standard-library web server and HTML/CSS/JavaScript frontend provide the demonstration interface.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible as an educational compiler project, provided the team clearly documents the supported subset and validates each compilation stage and the complete execution path.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Java Compiler and Bytecode Generation Engine\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team07_conceptual_workflow.jpg\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. N D Kulenavar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B07-1","PRJ-B07",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall accept supported Java source code.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Source input for the compiler pipeline.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall use Flex for lexical analysis and Bison grammar rules for parsing.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"E1 front-end design.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall keep tokens and intermediate compiler representations in memory rather than storing them in files or a database.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"In-memory compiler pipeline.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall perform semantic analysis and construct/validate the program representation.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"E1 front-end checks.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall generate and optimize an intermediate representation (IR).\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"E2 back-end optimization.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall generate JVM bytecode from optimized IR using ASM.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"E3 object-code generation.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall use the existing JVM to verify/load and execute generated bytecode.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"E4 verification and execution.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall execute the generated bytecode on the JVM and display the program output and stage results.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"S0: educational end-to-end pipeline and live demonstration.\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The compiler shall produce accurate results for supported Java programs.\",\"cat\":\"Correctness\",\"target\":\"At least 95% of supported Java programs produce accurate results.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The compiler shall analyse and process Java programs of up to 2,000 lines within 2 seconds under normal operating conditions.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Up to 2,000 lines within 2 seconds under normal operating conditions.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The compiler shall run on at least two operating systems: Windows and Linux.\",\"cat\":\"Portability\",\"target\":\"Runs on Windows and Linux.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Enter or load a restricted Java source program.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Provide source code → submit for compilation.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"File is missing/unreadable or contains unsupported input.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"View lexical tokens.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Submit valid source → lexer scans characters → tokens are shown.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid character or unterminated literal/comment.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Parse source and view AST.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Token stream → parser checks grammar → AST is constructed/displayed.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unexpected token or missing delimiter.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Run semantic analysis.\",\"mainFlow\":\"AST → symbol table/type checks → semantic result.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Undeclared, redeclared or type-incompatible variable.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Generate intermediate representation.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Validated AST → IR generation → IR displayed/stored for next stage.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Semantic errors prevent IR generation.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Generate JVM bytecode.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Validated IR → ASM generation → bytecode/class output.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unsupported construct or bytecode-generation error.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Execute generated program on JVM.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Generated class → JVM loads and executes → output displayed.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid class/bytecode or runtime failure.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"Student\",\"desc\":\"Inspect complete compiler pipeline.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Run compilation → view lexer, parser, semantic, IR, bytecode and output stages.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"A stage fails; system highlights the failed stage and message.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"A valid supported Java-subset program is accepted as input.\",\"method\":\"Run with sample programs containing supported constructs.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"All supported lexemes are classified into correct token categories with line numbers.\",\"method\":\"Compare token output against manually prepared expected tokens.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Parser receives the lexer token collection directly in memory; no token database/file is created.\",\"method\":\"Run compilation and verify no tokens.txt/compiler.db is required.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"A syntactically valid program produces a hierarchical AST; invalid syntax reports the source line.\",\"method\":\"Run valid and invalid syntax test cases.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Semantic analysis identifies undeclared/redeclared variables and incompatible types.\",\"method\":\"Run dedicated semantic error test cases.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"A semantically valid AST produces the defined IR representation.\",\"method\":\"Inspect IR for at least three valid programs.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Valid IR produces a JVM class/bytecode that passes JVM class loading.\",\"method\":\"Generate bytecode and load it with the JVM.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Generated programs execute and their output matches expected output.\",\"method\":\"Compare JVM output with expected results for test cases.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team07_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. N D Kulenavar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B07-2","PRJ-B07",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B07-3","PRJ-B07",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/java-compiler-bytecode\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B07-4","PRJ-B07",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A02-0","PRJ-A02",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"C Language Compiler & Code Generation Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A02-1","PRJ-A02",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A02-2","PRJ-A02",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A02-3","PRJ-A02",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A02-4","PRJ-A02",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B01-0","PRJ-B01",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Incremental Build & Dependency Resolution Tool\",\"problem\":\"Software projects often contain multiple build actions that depend on one another. When a source file changes, running every build action again can repeat work that is not required. Developers need a reliable way to identify the affected actions, respect dependency order and reuse previously generated outputs only when those outputs are still valid.\\n\\nThe proposed Incremental Build and Dependency Resolution Tool addresses this problem through file fingerprinting, dependency-graph analysis, build planning, task execution and artifact caching. The extended prototype also explores controlled execution of suitable independent actions on trusted computers connected through a local-area network (LAN).\",\"objectives\":[\"To create a project manifest describing build actions, commands, inputs, outputs and dependencies.\",\"To detect added, modified, deleted and unchanged files using SHA-256 fingerprints, and identify the build actions affected by those changes.\",\"To represent build actions as a directed dependency graph, detect dependency cycles and determine a valid execution order.\",\"To schedule and execute eligible actions as operating-system processes with bounded local parallelism, and investigate controlled TCP-based execution on trusted LAN workers.\",\"To reuse cached artifacts only when the complete declared action identity and expected outputs are valid, while persistently maintaining file state, artifact metadata and build history.\",\"To evaluate correctness and performance by comparing clean builds, incremental builds, cache reuse, and local versus networked execution.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Data Structures and Algorithms / Design and Analysis of Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Directed graphs, adjacency lists, hash maps/sets, BFS/DFS, topological sorting and cycle detection\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"File and process operations, process exit codes, worker pools, synchronization, concurrency limits and task-state management\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"SQLite tables, keys, CRUD operations, transactions, build history and persistent metadata\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Computer Networks\",\"concepts\":\"TCP client-server communication, message framing, worker registration, heartbeats, timeouts, task dispatch and artifact transfer\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Object-Oriented Programming\",\"concepts\":\"Interfaces and classes for adapters, actions, scheduler, cache, state repository, coordinator and workers\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[{\"id\":\"E1\",\"name\":\"Change Detection and Adapter Engine\",\"desc\":\"Load and validate the project manifest; scan configured files, calculate SHA-256 fingerprints and classify file changes.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"name\":\"Dependency and Impact Analysis Engine\",\"desc\":\"Represent build actions as a directed graph, identify affected actions, detect dependency cycles and determine a valid execution order.\"},{\"id\":\"E3\",\"name\":\"Scheduling, Execution, Cache and State Engine\",\"desc\":\"Schedule ready actions, execute build commands locally, validate and reuse eligible cached artifacts, and persist build state and results.\"},{\"id\":\"E4\",\"name\":\"Networked Worker Coordination Engine\",\"desc\":\"Register trusted LAN workers, dispatch eligible independent actions over TCP, transfer artifacts and validate returned results.\"}],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Command-line interface and documented project manifest.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"File scanning and SHA-256 fingerprints without requiring Git.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Dependency graph construction, impact analysis, cycle detection and execution planning.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Task scheduler with local process execution and bounded parallelism.\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Validated content-addressed artifact store or equivalent keyed cache.\"},{\"num\":\"6\",\"item\":\"SQLite persistence for metadata and build history; artifact bytes stored separately on disk.\"},{\"num\":\"7\",\"item\":\"Coordinator and a small number of trusted LAN workers communicating over TCP, subject to guide approval.\"},{\"num\":\"8\",\"item\":\"Limited, documented language adapters or build-command examples for simple reproducible test projects.\"},{\"num\":\"9\",\"item\":\"Logs and reports for changed files, selected actions, cache hits/misses, execution location, timing and failures.\"},{\"num\":\"10\",\"item\":\"Correctness tests and controlled performance benchmarks.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Universal support for every programming language, compiler, package manager or build configuration\",\"reason\":\"Beyond the manageable scope of the academic prototype.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Cloud-scale execution, public worker pools or internet-facing distributed execution\",\"reason\":\"Requires infrastructure and security controls outside the controlled LAN prototype.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Replacement for production build tools, package managers, CI platforms or source-control systems\",\"reason\":\"The project demonstrates selected build-system concepts, not a complete ecosystem replacement.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Execution of arbitrary tasks on untrusted worker machines\",\"reason\":\"The proposed worker model assumes explicitly configured trusted machines.\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Guaranteeing that every workload becomes faster when distributed\",\"reason\":\"Communication and transfer overhead may make small tasks slower.\"},{\"num\":\"6\",\"item\":\"Perfect automatic discovery of dynamic or hidden dependencies\",\"reason\":\"The supported prototype relies on declared manifest inputs and dependencies.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within mini-project scope\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with staged implementation. Graph algorithms, hashing, process execution, SQLite and TCP sockets are established techniques; local correctness should be completed before network execution.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"One coordinator computer and, if available, one or two additional computers connected to a controlled LAN are sufficient for a demonstrator. Local storage and a supported compiler/toolchain are required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic knowledge of data structures, graph algorithms, operating systems, databases, computer networks, object-oriented programming and command-line execution.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Limited development time; dependency declarations must be explicit; supported languages and action types must remain limited; LAN worker availability may vary.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Libraries may support parsing, hashing, database access and networking, while the core dependency analysis, build planning, cache-validation and scheduling logic must be implemented and studied by the team.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with scope control. The LAN execution extension requires project-guide approval before architecture freeze.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Incremental Build and Dependency Resolution Tool\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team_b01_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Dheerajkumar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B01-1","PRJ-B01",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Load and validate manifests with targets, actions, commands, inputs, outputs and dependencies.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Project manifest and build definitions.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Use SHA-256 to classify files as added, modified, deleted or unchanged.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Detect changes from the saved state.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Build the dependency graph: detect missing dependencies/cycles and select affected actions plus downstream dependencies.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Dependency analysis and action selection.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Schedule actions after prerequisites pass, within the configured concurrency limit.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Safe, bounded scheduling.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Run actions as OS processes: record exit codes, logs and duration.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Build execution and results.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Reuse artifacts only when the full action key matches and outputs validate.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Safe cache reuse.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"Persist file, action and artifact metadata and build history across runs in SQLite.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Consistent build state and history.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"Support LAN-worker registration, dispatch, heartbeat/timeouts and artifact fingerprint checks.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Proposed controlled-LAN extension; guide approval required.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"Report changed files, affected actions, cache activity, execution location, timings and failures.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Traceable build outcomes.\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Change classifications and affected-action sets must match expected test results.\",\"cat\":\"Correctness\",\"target\":\"100% of the defined file-change classification and dependency-graph test cases pass.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Incremental builds must match clean-build outputs for deterministic projects.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability / Correctness\",\"target\":\"Incremental and clean builds produce identical output hashes for 100% of the defined deterministic test projects.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Cache reuse must reject stale or mismatched artifacts.\",\"cat\":\"Cache safety\",\"target\":\"100% of tested cache-key mismatches and corrupted artifacts result in cache misses.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Execution must respect dependencies and concurrency limits.\",\"cat\":\"Dependency safety / Performance\",\"target\":\"Zero dependency-order violations; concurrent actions never exceed the configured limit.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Metadata and transferred artifacts must remain consistent and verifiable.\",\"cat\":\"Integrity / Fault tolerance\",\"target\":\"100% of defined restart and corruption tests preserve valid state and reject invalid artifacts.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Build activity and module interfaces must be traceable and documented.\",\"cat\":\"Observability / Maintainability\",\"target\":\"100% of executed build actions record status, duration and result; all defined core module interfaces are documented.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Load a project and request a target build.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select project/target → Load and validate manifest → Accept request.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid manifest or target → Show error; stop build.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Identify source-file changes.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Scan files → Hash with SHA-256 → Compare saved state → Classify changes.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unreadable file or missing input → Report path; never mark unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Find valid actions affected by changes.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Map changed files → Analyse dependencies → Select downstream closure → Validate graph.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Missing dependency or cycle → Report error; reject plan.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Reuse valid outputs from earlier builds.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Create action key → Check cache → Validate outputs → Reuse or schedule execution.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Key mismatch or invalid/missing output → Cache miss; execute action.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Developer / LAN Worker\",\"desc\":\"Execute eligible actions locally or on an approved worker.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Check prerequisites/limit → Run or dispatch task → Capture and verify result.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Action failure blocks dependants; worker timeout/invalid result is rejected.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Developer\",\"desc\":\"Inspect build results and execution history.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Record results → Show changes, actions, cache, location, timings and failures.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Incomplete report or interrupted build → Show available status and errors.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Valid manifests load; invalid or incomplete definitions are rejected with diagnostics.\",\"method\":\"Test valid, malformed and incomplete manifests.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"SHA-256 correctly classifies added, modified, deleted and unchanged files.\",\"method\":\"Test each change type against expected results.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"The graph detects missing dependencies/cycles and selects the complete affected-action closure.\",\"method\":\"Test valid, unresolved and cyclic graphs; compare selected actions.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Actions start only after prerequisites pass and never exceed the concurrency limit.\",\"method\":\"Run dependent/independent tasks; inspect states and timestamps.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Each process records exit code, output/error logs and elapsed time.\",\"method\":\"Run successful and failing commands; verify recorded results.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"Cache hits require a matching full action key and validated outputs.\",\"method\":\"Repeat unchanged build; alter inputs/configuration to test invalidation.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"File, action and artifact metadata and history persist consistently after restart.\",\"method\":\"Run builds, restart application and inspect stored records.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Approved workers register, receive tasks and return only verified artifacts; failures/timeouts are handled.\",\"method\":\"Test registration, dispatch, heartbeat, timeout and altered/incomplete transfers.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"Reports accurately show changes, affected actions, cache activity, execution location, timings and failures.\",\"method\":\"Compare reports with logs for cache, local/remote runs and failures.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team_b01_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Dheerajkumar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B01-2","PRJ-B01",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B01-3","PRJ-B01",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/incremental-build-system\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B01-4","PRJ-B01",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B13-0","PRJ-B13",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine\",\"problem\":\"In a multi-peer file distribution environment, a file may be divided into smaller portions and distributed among multiple participating peers for storage and transfer. Successful retrieval of the complete file therefore depends on the availability and accessibility of the required portions across the participating peers.\\n\\nIn practical distributed environments, peers may become temporarily unavailable due to process termination, network interruption or other failures. Such unavailability can make required file portions inaccessible and may interrupt file retrieval or prevent successful reconstruction. Repeatedly depending on the same unavailable peer can further increase recovery time and communication overhead.\\n\\nThe proposed Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine aims to study and develop a system that can continue file distribution and retrieval despite defined peer failures. The project focuses on identifying unavailable data, obtaining the required information from available participating peers, verifying recovered data and reconstructing the original file.\",\"objectives\":[\"To develop a multi-peer file distribution system capable of dividing and distributing file data among participating peers.\",\"To enable the system to identify unavailable or failed peers and determine the impact on ongoing file retrieval.\",\"To provide a recovery mechanism that can retrieve missing file data from available surviving replicas on participating peers and reconstruct the original file.\",\"To verify recovered data and successfully reconstruct the requested file after supported peer failures.\",\"To study the behaviour of the system under different controlled peer availability and failure conditions.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA)\",\"concepts\":\"Chunking, distribution strategy, replica/source selection, searching, ordering and reconstruction.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Computer Networks\",\"concepts\":\"Peer communication, data transfer, addressing, connection management and network failures.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Processes, process lifecycle, file handling, resource management and process failure.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Distributed Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Peer-based resource sharing, replication, fault tolerance, availability and failure handling.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems (DBMS)\",\"concepts\":\"Metadata storage, chunk-to-peer mapping, peer status and efficient metadata lookup.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"6\",\"course\":\"Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)\",\"concepts\":\"Modular system design using components such as Peer, Chunk, Transfer and Recovery.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"File chunking\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Multi-peer distribution\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Peer discovery / communication\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Peer failure simulation\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Missing-chunk detection\"},{\"num\":\"6\",\"item\":\"Chunk recovery + retry\"},{\"num\":\"7\",\"item\":\"Integrity + reconstruction\"},{\"num\":\"8\",\"item\":\"Recovery / performance evaluation\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Advanced Erasure Coding\",\"reason\":\"Reed-Solomon and other coding-based recovery techniques are proposed for future enhancement but are not implemented in the current prototype.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Dynamic Peer Scaling\",\"reason\":\"The prototype uses a predefined number of peers for controlled distribution, failure and recovery experiments.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Resumable Partial-Chunk Transfer\",\"reason\":\"Interrupted transfers are handled by retrying the complete chunk rather than resuming from the interrupted byte position.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Multiple Simultaneous Peer Failures Beyond Defined Scenarios\",\"reason\":\"Testing is limited to controlled failure scenarios so recovery behaviour can be measured consistently.\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Advanced Security and Access Control\",\"reason\":\"Authentication, authorization, encryption and enterprise-level security mechanisms are outside the core file distribution and recovery problem.\"},{\"num\":\"6\",\"item\":\"Graphical / Mobile User Interface\",\"reason\":\"The project focuses on the underlying distribution, failure detection, recovery, verification and reconstruction engines rather than interface development.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within scope\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with controlled scope and a limited number of participating peers.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"Student laptops/PCs, local network connectivity, file storage, development environment, version-control facility and multiple peer processes or systems for controlled experimentation.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA), Computer Networks, Operating Systems, Distributed Systems, DBMS and Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concepts.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Limited project duration, limited number of available machines/peers, controlled laboratory environment, bounded failure scenarios and the need to keep the implementation manageable within a mini-project.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Existing libraries and tools may be used for peripheral functions such as basic file handling, communication support and data verification. The core peer distribution, failure handling, recovery and reconstruction logic will be designed and studied by the team.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with controlled scope and a limited number of participating peers.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Resilient Multi-Peer File Distribution & Recovery Engine\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team13_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Pooja P Shettar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B13-1","PRJ-B13",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall allow a user to select a file for storage or transfer.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"File upload / distribution\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall divide the selected file into smaller fixed-size chunks for distributed storage.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Manageable distributed transfer\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall assign a unique identifier and sequence number to each generated chunk.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Chunk identification and correct reconstruction\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall generate and store a hash for each chunk to verify data integrity.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Detect corruption\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain membership information for participating peers.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Know available storage / transfer nodes\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall enable peers to communicate using TCP-based request and response messages.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Reliable peer communication\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall distribute file chunks across available peers.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Multi-peer file distribution\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall create and maintain replicas of chunks on multiple peers for fault tolerance.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Survive supported peer failures\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"The system shall detect peer failures using heartbeat responses and timeout conditions.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Detect unavailable peers\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"The system shall select an eligible surviving replica as the source for chunk recovery.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Recover data after a peer failure\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-11\",\"desc\":\"The system shall identify chunks that are unavailable, corrupted, or affected by a peer failure.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Locate affected data\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-12\",\"desc\":\"The system shall recover an affected chunk from a valid surviving replica.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Replica-based recovery\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-13\",\"desc\":\"The system shall detect interrupted transfers, discard incomplete data, and retry the affected chunk transfer.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Handle transfer interruption\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-14\",\"desc\":\"The system shall verify each recovered chunk by comparing its computed hash with the stored hash.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Validate recovered data\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-15\",\"desc\":\"The system shall reject invalid or corrupted recovery results and retry using another eligible source when available.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Prevent acceptance of corrupt chunks\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-16\",\"desc\":\"The system shall reconstruct the original file by ordering verified chunks according to sequence numbers and concatenating them.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Correct file retrieval\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-17\",\"desc\":\"The system shall report an unrecoverable condition when no valid surviving source is available.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Explicitly handle unrecoverable data\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-18\",\"desc\":\"The system shall record system events, failure detection, recovery activity, integrity results, retries, and performance metrics.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Evaluate reliability and performance\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall preserve successfully stored file data within the configured replication and supported peer-failure model. If no valid copy survives, it shall report the file/chunk as unrecoverable rather than silently claiming success.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"100% of tested unrecoverable cases are explicitly reported; no failed recovery is reported as successful.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall verify every received/recovered chunk against its stored integrity hash before accepting it.\",\"cat\":\"Integrity\",\"target\":\"100% of accepted chunks pass hash verification; a deliberately modified chunk is rejected in the hash-mismatch test.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall recover affected chunks after supported peer failures when at least one valid replica remains reachable.\",\"cat\":\"Fault tolerance\",\"target\":\"Initial target: successful recovery in ≥95% of eligible test cases. Exclude cases where no valid replica survives; validate with repeated controlled trials.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall mark an unresponsive peer unavailable within the specified detection target under the agreed test configuration.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Initial target: ≤2 seconds from simulated peer failure to unavailable-state notification. Record heartbeat interval, timeout, peer count and network conditions.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall measure recovery latency and report it by file size and peer count; a fixed pass threshold shall be set after baseline experiments.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Record elapsed time from failure/missing-chunk detection to verified chunk restoration for every trial. Report median and 95th percentile; approve a pass threshold only after baseline experiments.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain modular interfaces so each of the six engines can be tested independently and integrated through defined inputs/outputs.\",\"cat\":\"Maintainability / modularity\",\"target\":\"E1–E6 each pass component tests; integration tests verify the documented engine interfaces.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall produce repeatable outcomes for the same input files, configuration and peer-state sequence.\",\"cat\":\"Repeatability\",\"target\":\"Across repeated runs with the same configuration, chunk boundaries, sequence ordering and correctness outcome are identical. Placement is deterministic when peer order is unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall reconstruct a file without missing, duplicated or reordered verified chunks.\",\"cat\":\"Correctness\",\"target\":\"100% byte-for-byte equality between original and reconstructed files for every successful reconstruction test, verified by file hash and/or byte comparison.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Upload file for distribution\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select file; system chunks it, assigns IDs/sequences, hashes chunks and starts distribution.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid/unreadable file: reject with an error; do not mark upload successful.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Peer\",\"desc\":\"Join/register in network\",\"mainFlow\":\"Peer supplies IP/port and joins; membership information is updated.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Duplicate/unreachable peer: reject or mark unavailable; retain accurate membership state.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Peer\",\"desc\":\"Announce availability\",\"mainFlow\":\"Peer announces it can participate; status is reflected in peer metadata.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No response or disconnect: E4 eventually marks peer unavailable after timeout.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"System / Peer\",\"desc\":\"Distribute and store chunks\",\"mainFlow\":\"E3 selects peers using round-robin placement and stores configured replicas.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Target peer unavailable: do not record successful placement; retry or report placement failure.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"User\",\"desc\":\"Request file for retrieval\",\"mainFlow\":\"User requests file; system finds chunk metadata and requests chunks from peers.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Chunk missing or peer unavailable: invoke recovery from another eligible replica.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"Verify chunk integrity\",\"mainFlow\":\"Compute hash of received chunk and compare with stored hash.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Hash mismatch: reject chunk and try another eligible source.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"System / Peer\",\"desc\":\"Handle missing chunk\",\"mainFlow\":\"Identify affected chunk, look up replicas, request from a surviving peer and verify.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No valid surviving replica: report unrecoverable condition.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"Reconstruct file\",\"mainFlow\":\"Order verified chunks by sequence number and concatenate them.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Missing/duplicate sequence or incomplete set: do not report successful reconstruction.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"System / Maintainer\",\"desc\":\"Review monitoring and evaluation\",\"mainFlow\":\"Record detection/recovery events, integrity results, retries, bytes and timings.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Logging failure: report the limitation; do not silently claim metrics were captured.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"A supported test file can be selected; unreadable or missing input is rejected with a clear error.\",\"method\":\"Manual/UI test; valid and invalid file cases.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Chunks cover the input bytes exactly once; all chunks except possibly the last have the configured fixed size.\",\"method\":\"Chunk-size and byte-coverage test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Every chunk has a unique ID within the file and a sequence number that reflects original order.\",\"method\":\"Metadata uniqueness/order assertion.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"A hash is stored for every generated chunk and is reproducible for unchanged chunk bytes.\",\"method\":\"Hash recomputation test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Joining and leaving peers updates membership state accurately.\",\"method\":\"Peer registration/membership test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"Peers can exchange a framed request and response over TCP; connection errors/timeouts are surfaced.\",\"method\":\"TCP communication test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Every chunk is assigned to an eligible peer and the placement map records the assignment.\",\"method\":\"Normal distribution test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Each chunk has the configured number of distinct replicas when enough eligible peers are available.\",\"method\":\"Replica-count and placement test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"Under the agreed test configuration, a peer that stops responding is marked unavailable within the initial ≤2-second target.\",\"method\":\"Heartbeat/timeout test; record elapsed time, heartbeat interval, timeout and network conditions.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-10\",\"criteria\":\"Recovery selects a source whose peer is available and whose metadata identifies a replica of the required chunk.\",\"method\":\"Source-selection test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-11\",\"criteria\":\"The system identifies chunks on failed peers and detects missing chunks or hash mismatches.\",\"method\":\"Peer-failure and hash-mismatch tests.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-12\",\"criteria\":\"For a chunk with a valid surviving replica, the recovered bytes match the stored chunk hash.\",\"method\":\"Single-peer failure/recovery test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-13\",\"criteria\":\"After a transfer is interrupted, partial temporary data is discarded and a complete chunk transfer is retried.\",\"method\":\"Interrupt transfer mid-chunk; inspect retry and output.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-14\",\"criteria\":\"No recovered chunk is accepted unless its computed hash matches the stored hash.\",\"method\":\"Hash-mismatch test; accepted corrupt chunks = 0.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-15\",\"criteria\":\"A bad source is rejected; another eligible source is attempted; if none exists, unrecoverable status is reported.\",\"method\":\"Corrupt first replica / exhaust sources test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-16\",\"criteria\":\"Successful reconstruction is byte-for-byte identical to the original file.\",\"method\":\"Compare original and reconstructed files by bytes/hash.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-17\",\"criteria\":\"When no valid source survives, the system reports unrecoverable status and does not claim success.\",\"method\":\"No-surviving-source test.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-18\",\"criteria\":\"Logs include events, failure detection, recovery, integrity, retries, bytes transferred and relevant timings.\",\"method\":\"Inspect logs against generated test events.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team13_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Pooja P Shettar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B13-2","PRJ-B13",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B13-3","PRJ-B13",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/p2p-file-distribution-engine\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B13-4","PRJ-B13",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B14-0","PRJ-B14",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Adaptive Video Streaming & Buffer Management Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B14-1","PRJ-B14",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B14-2","PRJ-B14",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B14-3","PRJ-B14",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B14-4","PRJ-B14",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A07-0","PRJ-A07",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Network Traffic Monitoring & Protocol Analysis Tool\",\"problem\":\"Network administrators use packet-capture utilities, connectivity tests and interface statistics to understand network behaviour. However, a packet capture taken at only one location gives limited visibility into what happens along the complete network path. An observation at the sending point cannot by itself show whether the same traffic was delayed, changed or failed to appear at a later observation point. Synthetic tests such as ICMP ping may also not represent the behaviour of real application traffic. In addition, heavy traffic can make monitoring itself more difficult because capture and processing may not keep up with incoming packets.\\n\\nMini-Shark is proposed as a network traffic monitoring and protocol analysis tool that observes live traffic at more than one point in a controlled network, compares the observations and presents useful diagnostic information. It aims to help an operator investigate transit delay and unmatched observations while making clear that a missing observation may also result from capture or telemetry limitations, rather than automatically proving network-path loss. The proposed work will be demonstrated in a controlled virtual laboratory.\",\"objectives\":[\"To study how network traffic can be observed at multiple points along a communication path.\",\"To develop a tool that captures and analyses selected live network traffic for monitoring purposes.\",\"To compare packet observations from different points and estimate transit delay where timestamps are comparable.\",\"To identify unmatched observations for further investigation without treating them automatically as confirmed network loss.\",\"To evaluate the monitoring tool under normal, concurrent, delayed and high-traffic test conditions.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Computer Networks\",\"concepts\":\"Network communication, Ethernet and IP addressing, TCP/UDP, routing, packet behaviour and network performance.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Operating System Principles\",\"concepts\":\"Network interfaces, packet capture, processes and threads, producer-consumer concepts, buffering and resource management.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Data Structures & Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Efficient organization and comparison of packet observations, searching, matching and time-based processing concepts.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Organizing, storing and retrieving probe observations, monitoring events and diagnostic summaries.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Set up a controlled virtual network environment for client-to-server traffic experiments.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Observe selected live network traffic at two defined points in the communication path.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Analyse supported packet/protocol information and compare observations from the two points.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Present monitoring information such as observed traffic, transit-delay estimates and unmatched observations for investigation.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Monitoring every protocol and every possible network technology\",\"reason\":\"The project is limited to a manageable set of protocols and a controlled laboratory environment.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Deployment and monitoring of a large production or enterprise network\",\"reason\":\"The mini-project is intended to demonstrate the core concepts in a small, reproducible virtual lab.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Guaranteeing detection of every packet loss event or identifying the exact faulty router\",\"reason\":\"An unmatched observation alone cannot prove network-path loss; capture, telemetry and path effects must also be considered.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"The project is feasible as a bounded demonstration using four Ubuntu virtual machines in VMware Workstation. The work can be divided into lab setup, traffic observation, packet analysis, comparison and presentation of results.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"A computer capable of running VMware Workstation and four Ubuntu VMs, isolated virtual networks, test client/server applications and ordinary network-testing utilities. No dedicated physical network appliance is required for the proposed virtual-lab demonstration.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic Computer Networks, Linux and Operating System concepts, programming, Data Structures and Algorithms, and introductory DBMS and software-development concepts.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"Available host CPU and memory, VM scheduling and virtual-network behaviour may affect measurements. Probe clocks must be comparable for transit-delay estimates. Packet fields can change across routing, and NAT, fragmentation, retransmissions, capture offloads or telemetry failures can complicate observation matching.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Existing operating-system packet-capture facilities and standard network utilities may support capture and testing. The team should understand and implement the core observation comparison and diagnostic logic rather than treating a packet-analysis utility as the complete project.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible with a controlled four-VM scope and clearly documented measurement limitations.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Mini-Shark Network Traffic Monitoring & Protocol Analysis Tool\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team_a07_conceptual_workflow.jpg\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Pooja P Shettar","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A07-1","PRJ-A07",1,"Submitted","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall capture live packets at both configured probes\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Multi-point live packet capture\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall timestamp packets and extract supported protocol fields.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Timestamping and protocol analysis\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall generate deterministic 64-bit BLAKE2b packet fingerprints.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Packet fingerprint generation\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall send probe metadata to the Host VM over TCP port 9000.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Centralized probe telemetry\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall match probe observations using fingerprints within 500 ms.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Cross-probe packet matching\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall calculate transit delay and flag unmatched packets as suspected loss.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Delay and loss diagnosis\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall store results in SQLite and display them on the dashboard.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Persistent storage and monitoring\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall use bounded packet buffers and count queue overflows.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Bounded buffering and overload visibility\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall sustain the specified traffic load.\",\"cat\":\"Throughput / Bandwidth\",\"target\":\"≥10 Mbps for 5 minutes.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall process packets without unbounded queue growth..\",\"cat\":\"Processing Performance\",\"target\":\"≥1,000 packets/sec for 5 minutes.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall deliver probe telemetry within the specified time.\",\"cat\":\"Telemetry Latency\",\"target\":\"≤100 ms one-way.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall expire unmatched observations within the specified time.\",\"cat\":\"Correlation Latency\",\"target\":\"500 ms ± 50 ms.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall update dashboard results promptly.\",\"cat\":\"Dashboard Latency\",\"target\":\"≤2 seconds.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain a fixed-capacity buffer per probe.\",\"cat\":\"Memory / Buffer Capacity\",\"target\":\"10,000 slots per probe.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain synchronized probe clocks for delay measurement.\",\"cat\":\"Timing Accuracy\",\"target\":\"Clock offset ≤1 ms.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support concurrent traffic and telemetry without crashing.\",\"cat\":\"Concurrency / Availability\",\"target\":\"30 minutes continuous operation.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Operator\",\"desc\":\"The operator shall configure and start packet capture on the selected probe interfaces.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select probe and interface → apply capture settings → start/stop capture → view capture status.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid interface or insufficient privileges → display a capture error.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Client VM\",\"desc\":\"The client shall generate controlled TCP/HTTP traffic for monitoring across the virtual network.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Start test traffic → traffic traverses the Host VM routing function → Server VM receives traffic.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Server unreachable or test service stopped → report connectivity/test failure.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Probe Agent\",\"desc\":\"Each probe shall capture, timestamp, dissect and fingerprint packet observations.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Capture packet → timestamp → dissect supported headers → generate configured fingerprint → enqueue observation.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Unsupported/malformed packet or full buffer → record the applicable parse/overflow outcome.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Central Host\",\"desc\":\"The central service shall receive telemetry and correlate observations from both probes.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Receive telemetry → validate observation → correlate fingerprint → calculate delay or expire unmatched observation.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Telemetry missing/late or clocks unsynchronized → record telemetry issue or mark delay unreliable.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Administrator\",\"desc\":\"The administrator or analyst shall inspect monitoring status and diagnostic results.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Open dashboard/API → view probe status, packet matches, delay, unmatched observations and available counters → review stored results.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"API/database unavailable → display a service/data error rather than stale status.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Both configured probes capture live packets at their selected interfaces in the four-VM lab.\",\"method\":\"Generate client-to-server traffic and verify packet observations at Client-A and the Server VM.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Each captured observation includes a timestamp and correctly extracted fields for the supported protocols.\",\"method\":\"Generate Ethernet/IPv4 TCP, UDP, ICMP and DNS test traffic; compare extracted fields with an independent packet analyzer.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Identical configured packet-field inputs produce identical 64-bit BLAKE2b fingerprints at both probes.\",\"method\":\"Provide matching normalized field inputs to both probe implementations and compare the resulting fingerprints.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Both probes deliver observation metadata to the Host VM using TCP port 9000, and a telemetry failure is recorded if delivery is interrupted.\",\"method\":\"Verify delivery at the Host, then stop the telemetry receiver and verify that the probe/central service reports the failure.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"Corresponding observations are matched by fingerprint within the 500 ms correlation window.\",\"method\":\"Replay paired observations with controlled arrival intervals and verify match decisions inside the configured window.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"Matched observations produce T2 − T1 delay when clocks are synchronized; expired unmatched observations are labelled suspected loss.\",\"method\":\"Synchronize probe clocks, replay paired and unpaired observations, and verify delay and unmatched classifications.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"SQLite retains diagnostic records and the dashboard/API displays probe status, matches, delay, unmatched observations and available drop counters.\",\"method\":\"Compare dashboard/API output with SQLite records and verify persistence after an application restart.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Each probe buffer is limited to 10,000 slots and application-level overflow is counted when the queue is full.\",\"method\":\"Run a producer/consumer overload test and verify the configured capacity and overflow counter.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team_a07_use_case_diagram.png\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A07-2","PRJ-A07",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A07-3","PRJ-A07",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/network-traffic-monitoring\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A07-4","PRJ-A07",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A05-0","PRJ-A05",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Distributed Peer Search & File Sharing Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A05-1","PRJ-A05",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A05-2","PRJ-A05",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A05-3","PRJ-A05",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A05-4","PRJ-A05",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B11-0","PRJ-B11",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"DNS Resolver & Caching Server\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B11-1","PRJ-B11",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B11-2","PRJ-B11",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B11-3","PRJ-B11",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B11-4","PRJ-B11",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B12-0","PRJ-B12",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching\",\"problem\":\"When several users on the same network access the same web resource, each request may travel to the origin server even when the resource has not changed. This repeated communication increases response time and uses network bandwidth unnecessarily. In places such as college labs, hostels and shared networks, many users may request the same pages or static resources.\\n\\nOur proposed HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching is intended to reduce this repetition by placing a proxy between the client and the origin server. The proxy receives a request and checks whether a suitable, fresh response is already available in its memory. If it is available, the response can be returned to the client without contacting the origin server again. If it is not available or is no longer fresh, the proxy contacts the origin server and forwards the response to the client.\",\"objectives\":[\"To study how repeated requests for unchanged web resources affect response time and network bandwidth.\",\"To develop an HTTP proxy that accepts client requests and communicates with the required origin server.\",\"To reuse suitable cached responses for repeated requests while ensuring that only fresh responses are served.\",\"To reduce unnecessary communication with origin servers and study the effect of caching on response time and bandwidth usage.\",\"To apply Computer Networks, algorithm design and Operating Systems concepts in a practical networking system and evaluate its behaviour under multiple client requests.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Computer Networks\",\"concepts\":\"Client-server communication, TCP sockets, ports, HTTP request-response flow and proxy communication.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Design & Analysis of Algorithms (DAA)\",\"concepts\":\"Efficient searching, average-case lookup, cache replacement decisions and time/space complexity.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Operating System Principles\",\"concepts\":\"Processes and threads, concurrent request handling, synchronization, shared resources and resource management.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Software Engineering\",\"concepts\":\"Requirement analysis, modular development, testing, benchmarking, documentation and version control.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Accept client HTTP GET requests and forward supported requests to the intended destination server.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Check whether a requested resource has a suitable response available in the proxy's memory.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Return a valid cached response when available, and contact the origin server when the cache cannot satisfy the request.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Manage stored responses, their freshness and the removal of less recently used entries when the cache reaches its limit.\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Handle multiple client requests and evaluate request handling, cache reuse and response-time behaviour.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"HTTPS interception or decryption.\",\"reason\":\"The project focuses on controlled HTTP caching; decrypting HTTPS would introduce certificate and security complexity.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Distributed or multi-server proxy caching.\",\"reason\":\"The approved scope is a single-node proxy and does not require coordination between multiple proxy servers.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"AI/ML-based caching or traffic prediction.\",\"reason\":\"The main focus is to demonstrate request handling and cache reuse using foundational Computer Science concepts.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Production-scale deployment for unrestricted public traffic.\",\"reason\":\"A production deployment would require extensive security, monitoring and infrastructure work beyond the mini-project scope.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Feasibility within 3-credit scope\",\"assessment\":\"The project is achievable within the mini-project duration by limiting the implementation to a single-node HTTP proxy, supported GET requests and an in-memory cache. The team can demonstrate the complete request flow using a controlled HTTP origin server.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Required computing / resources\",\"assessment\":\"Standard laptops, a C/C++ development environment, local or controlled HTTP origin server, browser or curl for sending requests, and tools for basic load and performance testing. No special hardware is required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Basic networking, TCP sockets, HTTP request-response concepts, data structures, algorithm analysis, threads, synchronization and software testing.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major constraints / limitations\",\"assessment\":\"HTTPS traffic is outside the project scope; only supported HTTP requests and cacheable responses will be handled. Cache memory, worker threads and concurrent connections must be bounded. Origin-server availability and network conditions may affect measurements.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Use of existing libraries / tools\",\"assessment\":\"Common development and testing tools may be used for compilation, request generation and benchmarking. The core proxy request flow, cache management and concurrency handling will be implemented and understood by the team.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Feasible within the mini-project scope, with the implementation restricted to a single-node HTTP proxy and controlled test conditions.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of HTTP Proxy Server with In-Memory Caching\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team12_conceptual_workflow.jpg\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Sadaf Anjum Savanur","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B12-1","PRJ-B12",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall accept client HTTP GET requests through a TCP listening endpoint.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Client access through an intermediary\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall parse supported requests and identify the method, host and path.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Correct request processing\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall create a request key and check the in-memory cache for a matching response.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Reuse of responses for repeated requests\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall serve a fresh cached response without contacting the origin server.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Reduced origin traffic and latency\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall fetch a cache MISS from the origin, return it to the client and cache it if eligible.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Completion of requests not in cache\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall maintain LRU ordering and evict the least recently used entry at capacity.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Bounded in-memory cache capacity\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall handle invalid requests, disconnections, origin failures and timeouts without crashing or leaking resources.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Controlled failure behaviour\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall respond faster to fresh cache hits than to origin-forwarded requests.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"HIT response time ≥50% lower than MISS.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall manage cache memory and lookups efficiently as entries grow.\",\"cat\":\"Resource Efficiency\",\"target\":\"≤100 MB memory; lookup <10 ms for 10,000 entries.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall handle concurrent requests and failures without crashing or corrupting the cache.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"≥100 concurrent requests; failure rate <1%; no deadlocks.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall sustain increasing request loads with repeatable measurements.\",\"cat\":\"Scalability\",\"target\":\"≥500 req/s; p95 <200 ms; HIT ratio ≥70%.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Client\",\"desc\":\"The client shall send an HTTP GET request through the proxy.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Configure proxy and send GET → proxy accepts TCP connection → request received → method, host, path parsed.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Connection refused or request incomplete/malformed → reject and clean up safely.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Client / Proxy\",\"desc\":\"The client shall receive a resource that is fresh in the cache.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Build request key → check cache → fresh entry found → update recent-use position → return cached response.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Entry expired or invalid → treat as MISS and continue to origin.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Proxy / Origin Server\",\"desc\":\"The client shall obtain a resource that is not in the cache.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Cache MISS → connect to origin → forward GET → receive response → return to client → store eligible response.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Origin unreachable, times out or incomplete → controlled error response and cleanup.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Proxy\",\"desc\":\"The proxy shall free space when the cache reaches capacity.\",\"mainFlow\":\"New eligible response → check capacity → evict LRU entry → store new entry.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Cache operation fails or concurrent access → shared state protected, handled safely.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Client / Proxy\",\"desc\":\"The proxy shall handle an invalid request or client disconnect.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Validate request → detect invalid input or disconnect → return error where possible → release resources.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Malformed request, missing host or unsupported method → reject without crashing.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Multiple Clients\",\"desc\":\"Multiple clients shall use the proxy at the same time.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Accept connections → worker threads process requests → synchronized cache access → responses returned.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Worker/resource limit reached → excess work handled gracefully; no cache corruption.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"The proxy accepts client TCP connections and receives HTTP GET requests on its listening port.\",\"method\":\"Send GET requests with curl or a browser configured to use the proxy; verify receipt.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Method, host and path are identified correctly; invalid or incomplete requests are rejected safely.\",\"method\":\"Send valid and malformed GET requests; verify parsed values and controlled rejection.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"Each request produces a request key and a cache lookup that returns HIT or MISS correctly.\",\"method\":\"Request the same and different URLs; verify lookup outcomes in logs.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"A fresh cached entry is returned without any connection to the origin server.\",\"method\":\"Request a cacheable resource twice; verify the origin sees only the first request.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"On a MISS the proxy fetches from the origin, returns the response and caches it if eligible.\",\"method\":\"Request an uncached resource; verify client response, origin contact and new cache entry.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"At configured capacity the LRU entry is evicted and the new entry stored.\",\"method\":\"Set a small capacity, access entries in a known order, insert a new one; verify LRU eviction.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"Malformed requests, disconnects, origin failures and timeouts do not crash the proxy; resources are released.\",\"method\":\"Send malformed requests, stop the origin, disconnect mid-request; verify handling and no leaked sockets.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"HIT response time is at least 50% lower than MISS response time.\",\"method\":\"Measure average HIT and MISS latency under identical conditions.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Memory ≤100 MB and average lookup <10 ms with 10,000 entries.\",\"method\":\"Populate 10,000 entries; measure memory and lookup time.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"≥100 concurrent requests, failure rate <1%, no corruption or deadlock.\",\"method\":\"Run a concurrent load test; verify failure rate and cache consistency.\"},{\"req\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"≥500 req/s, p95 <200 ms and HIT ratio ≥70% on a repeated workload.\",\"method\":\"Run a controlled repeated-request benchmark; compute throughput, p95 and HIT ratio.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Ms. Sadaf Anjum Savanur","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B12-2","PRJ-B12",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B12-3","PRJ-B12",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/http-proxy-cache\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B12-4","PRJ-B12",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A06-0","PRJ-A06",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling\",\"problem\":\"Preparing an academic timetable involves coordinating courses, sections, faculty members, classrooms, laboratories and time slots while satisfying multiple interdependent constraints. Factors such as faculty and room availability, prescribed weekly teaching hours, fixed breaks and consecutive laboratory sessions make the scheduling process particularly complex.\\n\\nManual or iterative scheduling often requires repeated conflict checks, as resolving one conflict may introduce another. A faculty member may be assigned to overlapping classes, a section may receive two courses in the same time slot, or a room may be allocated to more than one class simultaneously. These conflicts can require repeated timetable revisions and make it difficult to maintain consistent schedules for all stakeholders.\\n\\nThe proposed Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling Engine aims to develop a structured system that models academic entities and scheduling rules, generates timetables that satisfy mandatory constraints, considers preferential requirements where feasible, and reports diagnostic information when no feasible schedule can be produced. The primary stakeholders are the Timetable Coordinator, Department Head (HoD), faculty members, and students/sections. Secondary stakeholders include room and laboratory coordinators and academic administration.\",\"objectives\":[\"To develop a structured data model for courses, sections, faculty members, classrooms, laboratories and scheduling time slots.\",\"To identify, classify and represent mandatory (hard) and preferential (soft) scheduling constraints.\",\"To generate academic timetables that satisfy all mandatory constraints and eliminate resource-allocation conflicts.\",\"To incorporate faculty availability and workload considerations into the scheduling process.\",\"To provide consistent and synchronized timetable views for classes, faculty members and rooms.\",\"To generate meaningful conflict information and diagnostic feedback when no feasible timetable can be produced.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Data Structures and Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Backtracking, search strategies, conflict detection and optimization techniques.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems (DBMS)\",\"concepts\":\"Relational data modelling, SQL, CRUD operations, transaction management and scheduling records.\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Discrete Mathematics\",\"concepts\":\"Sets, relations, propositional logic, combinatorial modelling and constraint satisfaction problems (CSPs).\",\"role\":\"Dominant\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)\",\"concepts\":\"Classes, objects, encapsulation, abstraction and modular software design.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Scheduling principles, resource allocation and resource-conflict management.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"6\",\"course\":\"Software Engineering\",\"concepts\":\"Requirements analysis, UML modelling, feasibility assessment and software testing.\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Course and section management\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Faculty allocation and availability management\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Classroom and laboratory resource management\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Working days, time slots and academic periods\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Hard- and soft-constraint representation\"},{\"num\":\"6\",\"item\":\"Timetable generation\"},{\"num\":\"7\",\"item\":\"Conflict detection and validation\"},{\"num\":\"8\",\"item\":\"Faculty workload assessment and balancing\"},{\"num\":\"9\",\"item\":\"Synchronized timetable views\"},{\"num\":\"10\",\"item\":\"Conflict diagnostics\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Student attendance management\",\"reason\":\"The project is focused on timetable preparation and constraint validation, not attendance recording.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Examination timetable generation\",\"reason\":\"The supplied Gate 0 scope addresses academic/course timetables; examination scheduling is excluded.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Payroll and employee administration\",\"reason\":\"Faculty records are used for allocation and availability only; payroll and broader HR functions are unrelated to the core objective.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Comprehensive college ERP system\",\"reason\":\"The project is limited to timetable generation and associated academic resource management.\"},{\"num\":\"5\",\"item\":\"Timetable scheduling across multiple universities\",\"reason\":\"The defined implementation is intended for department-level academic timetable preparation.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Technical feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The project can be implemented using established programming technologies such as Python or Java, a relational DBMS and appropriate scheduling algorithms. It applies DAA, DBMS, OOP, Discrete Mathematics, Operating Systems and Software Engineering concepts.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Operational and resource feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The system is intended to support routine, department-level timetable preparation using standard student laptops, commonly available development tools and representative academic scheduling data. Specialized hardware is not required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Time and scope constraints\",\"assessment\":\"The team has a 15-week semester and limited development resources. Priority should be given to feasible timetable generation, hard-constraint validation, conflict detection and consistent timetable views. Advanced optimization is secondary.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Prerequisite knowledge\",\"assessment\":\"Data Structures and Algorithms, Database Management Systems, Discrete Mathematics, Object-Oriented Programming, Operating Systems and Software Engineering.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Major project risks\",\"assessment\":\"Constraint combinations may produce no feasible solution; input data may be incomplete or inaccurate; resource mappings may be incorrect; scope expansion or development delays may occur.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Risk mitigation\",\"assessment\":\"Use early feasibility checks, systematic input validation, prioritization of mandatory constraints and phased implementation.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"GO WITH MODIFICATIONS. The project is feasible provided its scope remains focused on essential timetable generation and constraint validation.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Fig 1. Conceptual Workflow of Constraint-Based Academic Timetable Scheduling\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team06_conceptual_workflow.png\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Mallikarjun Akki","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A06-1","PRJ-A06",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"The system shall enable authorized users to create, import, and update academic scheduling data, including courses, faculty members, rooms, sections, batches, and related information.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Maintain academic data and the weekly calendar in a centralized location.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"The system shall allow users to configure working days, working hours, breaks, lunch periods, and time-slot rules before timetable generation.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Maintain academic data and the weekly calendar in a centralized location.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall validate all required input data before timetable generation and identify missing, inconsistent, or incompatible scheduling information.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Identify incomplete or invalid data before scheduling.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall represent and enforce all mandatory constraints, including faculty, room, section, and batch availability; room suitability and capacity; and time-continuity requirements.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Generate a clash-free timetable across faculty, rooms, sections, and batches.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall generate a timetable by assigning every required academic session to an eligible day, time slot, and room while satisfying all mandatory constraints.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Generate a clash-free timetable across faculty, rooms, sections, and batches.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support laboratory scheduling at the batch level, allowing different batches within the same section to use separate, suitable rooms concurrently when permitted by the scheduling constraints.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Generate a clash-free timetable across faculty, rooms, sections, and batches.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system shall evaluate and rank feasible timetable alternatives using configurable soft constraints, such as minimizing student gaps, avoiding repeated scheduling of the same subject on a day, balancing faculty workload, and improving room utilization.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge / Stretch\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Improve timetable quality by reducing gaps and balancing workload.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide actionable diagnostic information when the scheduling problem is infeasible or constraints cannot be satisfied. It shall also independently validate each generated timetable before assigning it a valid status.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Identify the reasons when timetable generation is unsuccessful.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"The system shall provide master, faculty-wise, section-wise, and batch-wise timetable views and support the export or sharing of timetable information in a suitable format.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"View and print timetables by section, faculty, and room.\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Every timetable accepted by the system shall be free from violations of mandatory (hard) constraints.\",\"cat\":\"Correctness\",\"target\":\"All hard-constraint validation checks (100%) must pass before a timetable is marked feasible.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Timetable generation shall complete within a predefined execution limit for the agreed reference dataset.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"The system shall either generate a result or terminate with a diagnostic status within 30 seconds on the agreed reference dataset.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"The system shall handle infeasible scheduling inputs without entering an indefinite execution state.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"Every generation attempt shall terminate with one of the following statuses: feasible, infeasible, or execution limit reached.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"The system shall support the agreed reference workload while preserving all academic and scheduling relationships.\",\"cat\":\"Scalability\",\"target\":\"The reference test dataset shall include at least 30 courses, 20 faculty members, 10 rooms, 4 sections, and their associated laboratory batches.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"The system shall communicate input and scheduling errors clearly and consistently.\",\"cat\":\"Usability\",\"target\":\"Every rejected or unsuccessful operation shall return a specific error message or diagnostic category.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"The system shall protect academic data and scheduling operations against unauthorized access or modification.\",\"cat\":\"Security\",\"target\":\"In the production environment, only authenticated and authorized users shall be permitted to modify configuration or timetable data.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"The system architecture shall support independent testing and traceability of requirements and scheduling-engine responsibilities.\",\"cat\":\"Maintainability\",\"target\":\"Every mandatory functional requirement shall be mapped to at least one acceptance test and one primary responsible engine component.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"UC-01\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Import or enter academic scheduling data.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select the data source; enter or upload the required information; allow the system to parse and store the data; review the validation summary.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Missing, invalid, or duplicate records are identified and reported. Such records are not treated as valid input.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-02\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Configure the academic calendar and time-slot rules.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Specify working days, working hours, breaks, and lunch periods; allow the system to derive the permitted time slots; review and confirm the configuration.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid, overlapping, or out-of-range time rules are rejected with an appropriate explanation.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-03\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Validate scheduling inputs before timetable generation.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Initiate validation; execute relationship, capacity, and configuration checks; display the validation status and any identified issues.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Any unresolved mandatory issue prevents timetable generation until the issue is corrected.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-04\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Generate a timetable using validated input data.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Initiate timetable generation; evaluate candidate assignments against the constraints; return the generated timetable when a feasible solution is found.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"If no feasible solution is found within the execution limit, return an infeasible or limit-reached status with relevant diagnostics.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-05\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Review infeasibility and scheduling-conflict diagnostics.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Review the diagnostic report; identify the affected constraint or resource category; update the relevant academic data or configuration.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"If the system cannot isolate the exact cause, it shall report the termination reason and the affected constraint or resource category.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-06\",\"actor\":\"Coordinator\",\"desc\":\"View the master timetable.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select a validated timetable generation; open the master timetable view; review the overall academic schedule and assignments.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"If no validated timetable is available, display a clear unavailable-state message.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-07\",\"actor\":\"Coordinator\",\"desc\":\"View an individual faculty timetable.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select a faculty member; retrieve the corresponding validated assignments; display the faculty member's weekly schedule.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"If the faculty member is unknown or has no assigned sessions, display an appropriate empty-state message.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-08\",\"actor\":\"Coordinator\",\"desc\":\"View section-wise and batch-wise timetables.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select a section or batch; retrieve the corresponding validated assignments; display theory and laboratory sessions at the appropriate level.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Laboratory sessions assigned to individual batches shall not be incorrectly represented as sessions for the entire section.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-09\",\"actor\":\"Coordinator\",\"desc\":\"Export or share a validated timetable.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select the required timetable view and export option; generate the corresponding shareable or downloadable representation.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"If no validated timetable exists, the export operation shall be disabled or rejected with a clear message.\"},{\"id\":\"UC-10\",\"actor\":\"Scheduler\",\"desc\":\"Regenerate the timetable after modifying constraints or academic data.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Modify the relevant data or constraints; revalidate the inputs; initiate generation; retain or version the previously validated timetable.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"A new infeasible generation attempt shall not overwrite or remove the previously validated timetable.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"FR ID-01\",\"criteria\":\"Academic data can be created or imported successfully, and all required entities and their relationships are available for validation.\",\"method\":\"Perform create/import tests and verify entity counts, completeness, and relationships.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-02\",\"criteria\":\"Valid calendar configurations generate only permitted time slots, while invalid, overlapping, or out-of-range rules are rejected.\",\"method\":\"Conduct boundary-value tests using valid, overlapping, and out-of-range time configurations.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-03\",\"criteria\":\"The system identifies known missing or invalid inputs before generation and reports all mandatory validation issues.\",\"method\":\"Execute negative testing with deliberately incomplete, invalid, or inconsistent input data.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-04\",\"criteria\":\"Every timetable accepted by the system has zero violations across all mandatory constraints.\",\"method\":\"Run automated validation against every defined hard constraint.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-05\",\"criteria\":\"For a feasible reference dataset, each required academic session is assigned exactly once to a valid day, time slot, and room.\",\"method\":\"Perform end-to-end generation testing, followed by assignment-count and constraint-validation checks.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-06\",\"criteria\":\"Different laboratory batches may be assigned simultaneously to separate suitable rooms, while a single batch is never assigned overlapping sessions.\",\"method\":\"Execute batch-level laboratory scheduling tests, including concurrent assignments and overlap checks.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-07\",\"criteria\":\"When multiple feasible timetable alternatives are available, the system selects and ranks them using the configured soft-constraint scoring mechanism and makes the score available for review.\",\"method\":\"Compare controlled candidate timetables and verify the calculated scores and ranking.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-08\",\"criteria\":\"Infeasible or execution-limit outcomes provide diagnostic information. Every generated timetable is independently validated before being marked valid.\",\"method\":\"Test with an infeasible dataset and verify post-generation validation and status handling.\"},{\"req\":\"FR ID-09\",\"criteria\":\"The master, faculty, section, and batch views display consistent validated assignments, and exported data matches the selected timetable view.\",\"method\":\"Perform cross-view consistency checks and verify the exported timetable against the selected view.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig 2. System Use Case Diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team06_use_case_diagram.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Mallikarjun Akki","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A06-2","PRJ-A06",2,"Draft","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A06-3","PRJ-A06",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/academic-timetable-scheduler\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A06-4","PRJ-A06",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A04-0","PRJ-A04",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Resource Packing & Allocation Optimization Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A04-1","PRJ-A04",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A04-2","PRJ-A04",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A04-3","PRJ-A04",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A04-4","PRJ-A04",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B03-0","PRJ-B03",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Route Planning & Network Optimization Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B03-1","PRJ-B03",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B03-2","PRJ-B03",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B03-3","PRJ-B03",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B03-4","PRJ-B03",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B06-0","PRJ-B06",0,"Draft","{\"title\":\"Dynamic Task Assignment & Scheduling Engine\",\"problem\":\"\",\"objectives\":[],\"coursesTable\":[],\"pipelineTable\":[],\"enginesTable\":[],\"scopeInTable\":[],\"scopeOutTable\":[],\"feasibilityTable\":[],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B06-1","PRJ-B06",1,"Draft","{\"frTable\":[],\"nfrTable\":[],\"userStoryTable\":[],\"acceptanceTable\":[],\"useCaseCaption\":\"\",\"useCaseImage\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B06-2","PRJ-B06",2,"Locked","{\"architecturePoints\":[],\"architectureCaption\":\"\",\"architectureImage\":\"\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B06-3","PRJ-B06",3,"Locked","{\"implementation\":\"\",\"modules\":\"\",\"repo\":\"\",\"challenges\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-B06-4","PRJ-B06",4,"Locked","{\"testing\":\"\",\"test_results\":\"\",\"metrics\":\"\",\"evaluation\":\"\"}","","","{}","","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A03-0","PRJ-A03",0,"Approved","{\"title\":\"Smart Campus Lab and Resource Optimizer\",\"problem\":\"In many colleges, academic timetables and resource allocation are still managed manually, making it difficult to handle sudden changes, additional classes, and faculty unavailability. These challenges often result in scheduling conflicts, underutilized classrooms and laboratories, and uneven faculty workloads. Managing multiple scheduling requests is also complicated due to the lack of a systematic approach to prioritizing requests, ensuring fairness, and considering resource availability. Therefore, an efficient system is needed to simplify timetable management, optimize the use of existing resources, and ensure smooth academic activities with minimal disruption.\",\"objectives\":[\"To detect overlapping booking requests and resource conflicts accurately.\",\"To allocate resources while following capacity, time-slot and eligibility rules.\",\"To improve resource utilization and fairness compared with first-come-first-served allocation.\",\"To identify and report requests that cannot be fulfilled when resources are limited.\",\"To generate reports on resource utilization, fairness and conflicts.\",\"To test the system with increasing numbers of booking requests.\"],\"coursesTable\":[{\"sl\":\"1\",\"course\":\"Design & Analysis of Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Greedy algorithms, Dynamic Programming and Backtracking\",\"role\":\"Core\"},{\"sl\":\"2\",\"course\":\"Data Structures & Algorithms\",\"concepts\":\"Trees, heaps, graphs and interval-based conflict detection\",\"role\":\"Core\"},{\"sl\":\"3\",\"course\":\"Operating Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Concurrency, process scheduling, locks and resource coordination\",\"role\":\"Core\"},{\"sl\":\"4\",\"course\":\"Discrete Mathematical Structures\",\"concepts\":\"Sets, relations, graphs and constraint modelling\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"5\",\"course\":\"Software Engineering\",\"concepts\":\"Modular design, requirements and system testing\",\"role\":\"Supporting\"},{\"sl\":\"6\",\"course\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"concepts\":\"Booking records, data storage, transactions and data consistency\",\"role\":\"Core\"}],\"pipelineTable\":[{\"input\":\"Booking request with resource, date, time and requirements\",\"processing\":\"Check availability, capacity, eligibility and time conflicts\",\"userAction\":\"Confirm request, select an alternative or join waiting queue\",\"output\":\"Confirmed allocation or conflict/waiting status\"}],\"enginesTable\":[{\"id\":\"E1\",\"name\":\"Scheduling & Concurrency Controller\",\"desc\":\"Manages simultaneous requests using queues, priorities and locks.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"name\":\"Search, Conflict & Allocation Engine\",\"desc\":\"Finds available resources, detects clashes and selects suitable allocations.\"},{\"id\":\"E3\",\"name\":\"Data Management & Integrity Engine\",\"desc\":\"Stores booking and resource data and maintains accurate, consistent records.\"},{\"id\":\"E4\",\"name\":\"Control & Monitoring Dashboard\",\"desc\":\"Provides user dashboards to submit requests, view schedules and monitor resource usage.\"}],\"scopeInTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Model resources and booking rules such as capacity, time slots and eligibility.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Detect time-slot conflicts efficiently using interval-based algorithms.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Allocate resources using constraint-based or utilization-focused algorithms.\"},{\"num\":\"4\",\"item\":\"Report unavoidable conflicts, resource utilization and allocation fairness.\"}],\"scopeOutTable\":[{\"num\":\"1\",\"item\":\"Real-time IoT integration with cameras, RFID or occupancy sensors.\",\"reason\":\"Hardware integration is outside the project scope.\"},{\"num\":\"2\",\"item\":\"Full campus-wide deployment across multiple buildings.\",\"reason\":\"A pilot-scale software demonstration is sufficient.\"},{\"num\":\"3\",\"item\":\"Payment and billing integration.\",\"reason\":\"Not required for resource allocation.\"}],\"feasibilityTable\":[{\"aspect\":\"Technical Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Achievable using standard algorithms, data structures and open-source tools.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Operational Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The system can be tested using sample booking requests and simulated resource availability.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Time Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"The project is achievable within the 15-week semester.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Resource Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"A team of four can develop and test the system using standard laptops; no special hardware is required.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Constraints\",\"assessment\":\"Limited access to real booking data; testing will use synthetic datasets. The team has a 15-week timeline and must ensure correct conflict detection and fair allocation.\"},{\"aspect\":\"Overall Feasibility\",\"assessment\":\"Go – the software-based project is achievable within the available time, team size and resources.\"}],\"conceptualFlowCaption\":\"Conceptual Flow - Smart Campus Lab & Resource Optimizer\",\"conceptualFlowImage\":\"assets/team03_conceptual_flow.jpg?v=flow-2\"}","Stage 0 problem definition, scope, and feasibility approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Amit Kachavimath","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A03-1","PRJ-A03",1,"Approved","{\"frTable\":[{\"id\":\"FR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Allow coordinators to upload existing college timetables.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Reduce manual timetable preparation and updating.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Extract subjects, faculty, classrooms, laboratories, and time slots from uploaded timetables.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Organize timetable information in a usable form.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Detect overlapping classes, faculty conflicts, and room conflicts.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Prevent scheduling conflicts and booking clashes.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Check whether the required lecture, tutorial, and practical (L:T:P) hours are followed.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Ensure academic hour requirements are met.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"Allow students and faculty to view timetables and available time slots.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Provide clear timetable information and reduce confusion.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Allow faculty to request extra classes, schedule changes, or report unavailability.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Handle additional classes and faculty unavailability.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"Handle incoming requests based on priority, fairness, and resource availability.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Manage competing requests fairly and efficiently.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"Suggest available classrooms, laboratories, and suitable faculty members.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Improve use of available rooms, labs, and faculty time.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"Allow HODs to approve or reject scheduling requests.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Support controlled approval of scheduling changes.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"Update the timetable after approved changes.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Keep the timetable accurate after approved changes.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-11\",\"desc\":\"Generate reports showing classroom usage, laboratory usage, and faculty workload.\",\"priority\":\"Mandatory\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Monitor resource utilization and faculty workload.\"},{\"id\":\"FR ID-12\",\"desc\":\"Notify affected students and faculty about timetable changes.\",\"priority\":\"Challenge\",\"sourceNeed\":\"Keep students and faculty informed about schedule changes.\"}],\"nfrTable\":[{\"id\":\"NFR ID-01\",\"desc\":\"Timetables should load quickly.\",\"cat\":\"Performance\",\"target\":\"Within 3 seconds.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-02\",\"desc\":\"Avoid overlapping faculty and room allocations.\",\"cat\":\"Accuracy\",\"target\":\"Zero conflicts in approved schedules.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-03\",\"desc\":\"Scheduling requests should not be lost.\",\"cat\":\"Reliability\",\"target\":\"100% of accepted requests saved.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-04\",\"desc\":\"Requests should be handled fairly without unnecessary delays.\",\"cat\":\"Fairness\",\"target\":\"Track waiting time for every request.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-05\",\"desc\":\"System should be simple and easy to use.\",\"cat\":\"Usability\",\"target\":\"Basic tasks completed without training.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-06\",\"desc\":\"Only authorized users can modify schedules.\",\"cat\":\"Security\",\"target\":\"Access based on user roles.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-07\",\"desc\":\"System should support multiple semesters.\",\"cat\":\"Scalability\",\"target\":\"Initially support 1st, 3rd, and 5th semesters.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-08\",\"desc\":\"Scheduling rules should be easy to update.\",\"cat\":\"Maintainability\",\"target\":\"Change rules without modifying core code.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-09\",\"desc\":\"New scheduling requests should be processed quickly.\",\"cat\":\"Responsiveness\",\"target\":\"Suggest allocation within 5 seconds.\"},{\"id\":\"NFR ID-10\",\"desc\":\"Original timetable information should remain safe.\",\"cat\":\"Data Integrity\",\"target\":\"Maintain original and updated timetable versions.\"}],\"userStoryTable\":[{\"id\":\"01\",\"actor\":\"Student / Project Team\",\"desc\":\"User wants to view available labs, rooms or equipment.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Log in → Open resource list → Select date and time → System displays available resources.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No matching resource → System displays that no resource is available.\"},{\"id\":\"02\",\"actor\":\"Student / Project Team\",\"desc\":\"User wants to request a resource.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Select resource → Enter time slot, group size and requirements → Submit request.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Missing or invalid details → System asks the user to correct them.\"},{\"id\":\"03\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"System wants to validate a booking request.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Check time availability → Check capacity and eligibility → Check existing bookings.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Constraint not satisfied → System rejects the request or shows the reason.\"},{\"id\":\"04\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"System wants to detect conflicts between requests.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Compare time windows and resource usage → Identify overlapping requests → Mark conflict.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Booking data unavailable → System reports the validation failure.\"},{\"id\":\"05\",\"actor\":\"System\",\"desc\":\"System wants to allocate resources fairly.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Rank eligible requests → Apply priority and fairness rules → Select suitable resource → Confirm allocation.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Resource taken by another request → System retries allocation or places request in waiting queue.\"},{\"id\":\"06\",\"actor\":\"Student / Project Team\",\"desc\":\"User wants an alternative when the requested resource is unavailable.\",\"mainFlow\":\"System searches suitable alternatives → Displays available resources/time slots → User selects an option.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"No suitable alternative → User may join the waiting queue or leave the request unresolved.\"},{\"id\":\"07\",\"actor\":\"Faculty / Lab Instructor\",\"desc\":\"Faculty wants to review and manage booking requests.\",\"mainFlow\":\"View requests → Check details and availability → Approve or reject request.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Request violates resource rules → System displays the reason and does not confirm it.\"},{\"id\":\"08\",\"actor\":\"Lab / Facility Administrator\",\"desc\":\"Administrator wants to manage resource and booking information.\",\"mainFlow\":\"View resource records → Add or update resource details and constraints → Save changes.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Invalid details or conflicting update → System rejects the change.\"},{\"id\":\"09\",\"actor\":\"HOD / Administrator\",\"desc\":\"HOD or administrator wants to monitor resource usage.\",\"mainFlow\":\"Open dashboard → View utilization, waiting requests, conflicts and fairness reports.\",\"exceptionFlow\":\"Report data unavailable → System displays an appropriate status message.\"}],\"acceptanceTable\":[{\"req\":\"01\",\"criteria\":\"The system displays resources that are available for the selected date and time.\",\"method\":\"Create resource records with known availability and verify the displayed list.\"},{\"req\":\"02\",\"criteria\":\"The system accepts valid booking requests containing resource, time slot and group size details.\",\"method\":\"Submit valid and invalid booking requests and verify the response.\"},{\"req\":\"03\",\"criteria\":\"The system checks capacity, eligibility and time-window constraints before confirming a booking.\",\"method\":\"Test requests that satisfy and violate each constraint.\"},{\"req\":\"04\",\"criteria\":\"The system detects overlapping requests and does not confirm conflicting allocations.\",\"method\":\"Submit requests for the same resource and overlapping time slots.\"},{\"req\":\"05\",\"criteria\":\"The system allocates resources using the defined priority and fairness rules.\",\"method\":\"Run a set of competing requests and compare the allocation with the defined rules.\"},{\"req\":\"06\",\"criteria\":\"The system suggests suitable alternatives or places an unallocated request in the waiting queue.\",\"method\":\"Test requests when the preferred resource is unavailable and then free a resource.\"},{\"req\":\"07\",\"criteria\":\"The system stores confirmed bookings correctly, including when multiple requests are submitted at the same time.\",\"method\":\"Run simultaneous booking tests and verify stored booking records.\"},{\"req\":\"08\",\"criteria\":\"The system generates reports showing resource utilization, fairness and unresolved conflicts.\",\"method\":\"Compare dashboard and report values with the test booking data.\"}],\"useCaseCaption\":\"Fig : Use case diagram\",\"useCaseImage\":\"assets/team03_usecase_diagram_doc.png\"}","Requirements specification, user stories, and acceptance criteria approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Amit Kachavimath","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A03-2","PRJ-A03",2,"Approved","{\"architecturePoints\":[\"1. The Smart Campus Lab and Resource Optimizer uses a Layered Architecture with Microservices (Hybrid).\",\"2. Presentation Layer: Provides the user interface for students, faculty, HODs, and administrators.\",\"3. It includes features like timetable viewing, booking requests, reports, and admin approvals.\",\"4. API/Controller Layer: Handles REST API requests, request validation, routing, authentication, and role-based access.\",\"5. Business Layer: Contains eight microservices that handle the main operations of the system.\",\"6. These services include timetable import, conflict detection, L-T-P validation, request scheduling, and resource recommendation.\",\"7. It also includes approval workflow, reporting and analytics, and notification services.\",\"8. Data Access Layer: Manages the storage and retrieval of system data.\",\"9. It uses Supabase/PostgreSQL for permanent storage and an In-Memory Store for fast caching and session management.\",\"10. All four layers work together to provide efficient resource booking, conflict-free allocation, and better resource utilization.\"],\"architectureCaption\":\"Fig 1. Architecture Diagram\",\"architectureImage\":\"assets/team03_architecture_doc.png\",\"dataDesignPoints\":[\"1. This data design organizes the Smart Campus Lab and Resource Optimizer into four connected areas.\",\"2. Academic Structure represents departments, divisions, students, and enrollment records.\",\"3. Programs connect departments to divisions, while enrollment links students to their divisions.\",\"4. Shared Resources represents buildings, resource types, rooms, and available facilities.\",\"5. Resource Facility connects each resource to its facilities without duplicating facility records.\",\"6. Teaching Assignment links faculty members, subjects, and divisions.\",\"7. Timetable Entry records recurring teaching activities, while Activity Occurrence represents dated sessions.\",\"8. Timetable Change tracks modifications to scheduled activities through related change records.\",\"9. Usage Request records a user’s request, while Allocation identifies the resource assigned.\",\"10. Presence records are linked to allocations and users, separating actual attendance from planned usage.\",\"11. Approval supports decisions requiring authorization, while supporting tables hold additional academic and operational data.\",\"12. Primary keys identify records, foreign keys connect them, and relationship labels indicate how records are associated.\"],\"dataDesignCaption\":\"Fig 2. Data Design\",\"dataDesignImage\":\"assets/team03_datadesign_doc.jpeg\",\"algorithmDesignPoints\":[\"The process starts with Validation, where the booking request is checked for valid time, resource and type.\",\"The Interval Tree checks whether the requested time slot overlaps with existing bookings.\",\"It helps identify conflicting bookings efficiently without checking every booking.\",\"The Sweep-Line Algorithm calculates the maximum number of bookings happening at the same time.\",\"It checks whether the resource capacity is sufficient for the requests.\",\"Priority and Aging assigns priority to requests based on importance and waiting time.\",\"The Greedy Algorithm makes the final decision by accepting or rejecting a booking request.\",\"If the request satisfies all conditions, it is accepted; otherwise, it is rejected.\",\"If rejected, the system provides a list of ranked alternative resources or time slots.\",\"Dynamic Programming (DP) runs separately to find the best allocation when multiple pending requests need to be optimized together.\",\"Jain's Fairness Index is calculated later to measure how fairly resources are distributed among users.\"],\"algorithmDesignCaption\":\"Fig 3. Algorithm Design\",\"algorithmDesignImage\":\"assets/team03_algorithmdesign_new.jpeg\",\"securityDesignPoints\":[]}","Architecture, data design, and algorithmic modeling approved.",9,"{}","Mr. Amit Kachavimath","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A03-3","PRJ-A03",3,"Approved","{\"implementation\":\"Modular engine pipeline implemented following System Design boundaries.\",\"modules\":\"E1: Scheduling & Concurrency Controller; E2: Search, Conflict & Allocation Engine; E3: Data Management & Integrity Engine; E4: Control & Monitoring Dashboard\",\"repo\":\"https://github.com/topics/campus-resource-optimizer\",\"challenges\":\"Handled concurrency and edge-case validation across module boundaries.\"}","Core implementation modules verified.",9,"{}","Mr. Amit Kachavimath","2026-11-12T03:22:42.694Z"],["SUB-A03-4","PRJ-A03",4,"Approved","{\"testing\":\"Acceptance test suite covering all FR and NFR targets.\",\"test_results\":\"All acceptance verification checks passed.\",\"metrics\":\"Zero hard-constraint conflicts; target response latency met.\",\"evaluation\":\"Ready for final v